, hidden_size = hidden_states.shape\\n        # Check constants\\n        assert hidden_size == 4096\\n\\n        EPS = 1e-5\\n\\n        x = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n        inv_rms = torch.rsqrt(x.pow(2).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + EPS)\\n        y = (x * inv_rms) * weight.to(torch.float32)\\n        return y.to(hidden_states.dtype)\"\n44\t}\n45\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8588311-4b23-423c-adbc-8b67af18966a","uuid":"cfaf9c53-31d0-4c94-9322-38982489522b","timestamp":"2026-09-16T16:55:46.032Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--flashinfer--rmsnorm_h4096/task/definition.json","content":"{\n  \"name\": \"rmsnorm_h4096\",\n  \"op_type\": \"rmsnorm\",\n  \"description\": \"Root Mean Square Normalization with hidden_size=4096. Captured from Llama-3.1-8B. Epsilon is fixed at 1e-5.\",\n  \"tags\": [\n    \"status:verified\",\n    \"model:llama-3.1-8b\"\n  ],\n  \"axes\": {\n    \"batch_size\": {\n      \"type\": \"var\"\n    },\n    \"hidden_size\": {\n      \"type\": \"const\",\n      \"value\": 4096\n    }\n  },\n  \"inputs\": {\n    \"hidden_states\": {\n      \"shape\": [\n        \"batch_size\",\n        \"hidden_size\"\n      ],\n      \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\"\n    },\n    \"weight\": {\n      \"shape\": [\n        \"hidden_size\"\n      ],\n      \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"outputs\": {\n    \"output\": {\n      \"shape\": [\n        \"batch_size\",\n        \"hidden_size\"\n      ],\n      \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"reference\": \"import torch\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(hidden_states, weight):\\n    batch_size, hidden_size = hidden_states.shape\\n    # Check constants\\n    assert hidden_size == 4096\\n\\n    EPS = 1e-5\\n\\n    x = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    inv_rms = torch.rsqrt(x.pow(2).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + EPS)\\n    y = (x * inv_rms) * weight.to(torch.float32)\\n    return y.to(hidden_states.dtype)\",\n  \"reference_kb_format\": \"import torch\\nimport torch.nn as nn\\n\\nclass Model(nn.Module):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Root Mean Square Normalization with hidden_size=4096. Captured from Llama-3.1-8B. Epsilon is fixed at 1e-5.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        super(Model, self).__init__()\\n\\n    @torch.no_grad()\\n    def forward(self, hidden_states, weight):\\n        batch_size, hidden_size = hidden_states.shape\\n        # Check constants\\n        assert hidden_size == 4096\\n\\n        EPS = 1e-5\\n\\n        x = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n        inv_rms = torch.rsqrt(x.pow(2).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + EPS)\\n        y = (x * inv_rms) * weight.to(torch.float32)\\n        return y.to(hidden_states.dtype)\"\n}\n","numLines":45,"startLine":1,"totalLines":45}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SfMYdZ7JVywefHBbqYSWV1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — `rmsnorm_h4096` (FlashInfer, A800 / sm_80)\n2\t\n3\t## 0. Purpose & scope of this document\n4\t\n5\tThis is the analysis draft required before any code or executable plan. It captures:\n6\tthe operation semantics, the exact numerics to reproduce, the workload regimes,\n7\thardware roofline, the Triton design space, numerical risks, and a validation\n8\tstrategy given the tight feedback loop. No solution code and no `plan.md` are created\n9\there. The concrete, ordered candidate steps belong in `docs/plan.md` (next turn).\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## 1. Operation semantics\n14\t\n15\tRMSNorm (Root Mean Square Layer Normalization), row-wise over the hidden axis.\n16\t\n17\tReference (authoritative, from `task/definition.json`):\n18\t\n19\t```python\n20\t@torch.no_grad()\n21\tdef run(hidden_states, weight):\n22\t    batch_size, hidden_size = hidden_states.shape      # hidden_size == 4096 (asserted)\n23\t    EPS = 1e-5\n24\t    x = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)                # upcast bf16 -> fp32\n25\t    inv_rms = torch.rsqrt(x.pow(2).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + EPS)\n26\t    y = (x * inv_rms) * weight.to(torch.float32)       # scale, then weight; fp32 math\n27\t    return y.to(hidden_states.dtype)                   # downcast fp32 -> bf16\n28\t```\n29\t\n30\tPer row `i` (length `H = 4096`):\n31\t\n32\t```\n33\tms_i     = (1/H) * Σ_j x_ij^2            # mean of squares, fp32\n34\tinv_rms_i = 1 / sqrt(ms_i + 1e-5)        # fp32\n35\ty_ij     = (x_ij * inv_rms_i) * w_j      # fp32\n36\tout_ij   = bf16(y_ij)\n37\t```\n38\t\n39\tKey exact-semantics facts I must reproduce to stay inside tolerance:\n40\t\n41\t- **Accumulation dtype is fp32.** Input is bf16, upcast to fp32 *before* squaring.\n42\t- **Reduction denominator is `H = 4096`** (a true mean, not sum). Epsilon `1e-5` is\n43\t  added to the *mean of squares* (i.e. to `E[x^2]`), not to a Bessel-corrected\n44\t  variance and not to the RMS itself.\n45\t- **No mean-subtraction** — this is RMSNorm, not LayerNorm; there is no centering.\n46\t- **Multiply order:** `(x * inv_rms) * weight`, all in fp32; `weight` is upcast to\n47\t  fp32 first. Only the final result is cast back to bf16.\n48\t- **Epsilon is a fixed constant `1e-5`** (also stated in the description). Hardcode it.\n49\t\n50\t### Shapes / dtypes\n51\t- `hidden_states`: `[batch_size, 4096]`, **bf16**.\n52\t- `weight`: `[4096]`, **bf16**.\n53\t- `output`: `[batch_size, 4096]`, **bf16**.\n54\t- `hidden_size` is a compile-time constant (`4096`, a power of two → nice for tiling,\n55\t  no reduction-mask needed).\n56\t- `batch_size` is the only variable axis.\n57\t\n58\t---\n59\t\n60\t## 2. Workload analysis\n61\t\n62\tFive fixed feedback workloads (only `batch_size` varies):\n63\t\n64\t| uuid (short) | batch_size | rows | input bytes (bf16) | regime |\n65\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n66\t| 9d40…5001 | 15 | 15 | 15·4096·2 ≈ 120 KB | tiny / latency-bound |\n67\t| 1453… | 64 | 64 | ≈ 512 KB | small / latency-bound |\n68\t| 33bf… | 7 | 7 | ≈ 57 KB | tiny / latency-bound |\n69\t| 841b… | 14509 | 14509 | ≈ 113 MB | large / bandwidth-bound |\n70\t| f0f5… | 14418 | 14418 | ≈ 113 MB | large / bandwidth-bound |\n71\t\n72\t**Two clearly distinct regimes**, and the geomean is over both, so both matter:\n73\t\n74\t- **Tiny/small (7, 15, 64 rows).** Far below the ~108-SM A800's ability to fill.\n75\t  With \"one program per row\" the grid is 7–64 blocks → most SMs idle. But the total\n76\t  work is minuscule (tens–hundreds of KB), so wall-time is dominated by **kernel-launch\n77\t  latency and memory latency**, not throughput. The dominant win here is **fusion**:\n78\t  the torch reference launches many kernels and materializes fp32 intermediates,\n79\t  whereas a single fused Triton kernel is one launch reading bf16 once / writing once.\n80\t- **Large (14418, 14509 rows).** ~113 MB read + ~113 MB written ⇒ ~226 MB of HBM\n81\t  traffic (weight is 8 KB, cached, negligible). This regime is strictly\n82\t  **memory-bandwidth-bound**; the objective is to hit the bf16-in/bf16-out roofline\n83\t  and avoid the reference's extra fp32-intermediate traffic.\n84\t\n85\t**Implication:** a single, lean, fused, one-pass kernel should win in *both* regimes\n86\tagainst the un-fused reference. The optimization headroom is (a) not leaving bandwidth\n87\ton the table for the large cases and (b) minimizing overhead for the tiny cases.\n88\t\n89\t---\n90\t\n91\t## 3. Hardware roofline (A800, sm_80)\n92\t\n93\t- A800 = GA100 (108 SMs), sm_80, HBM2e. Peak HBM bandwidth ≈ **1.5–2.0 TB/s**\n94\t  (≈2.0 TB/s for the 80 GB SKU, ≈1.55 TB/s for the 40 GB SKU); 40 MB L2.\n95\t- Arithmetic intensity of RMSNorm is very low: ~2 FLOP-ish per element for the\n96\t  square + a couple of multiplies, against 2 bytes read + 2 bytes written per element.\n97\t  → **Memory bound.** FP32 ALU throughput is irrelevant at these sizes.\n98\t\n99\t**Ideal large-case time** (bf16 in + bf16 out, weight negligible):\n100\t`226 MB / 1.8 TB/s ≈ 125 µs` (≈100 µs at 2.0 TB/s, ≈145 µs at 1.55 TB/s).\n101\tThis is the target floor for batch≈14.5k. Any fused kernel that reads each input\n102\telement once and writes each output once, with coalesced accesses, should approach it.\n103\t\n104\t**Reference inefficiency:** the torch reference upcasts to fp32 (`x = …to(float32)`\n105\tis a 4096·B·4-byte materialization = 2× the input traffic at fp32), then `x.pow(2)`,\n106\tthen `.mean(...)`, then broadcast-mul twice, then downcast — multiple passes over\n107\tlarge fp32 buffers plus several kernel launches. So the *speedup vs reference* on the\n108\tlarge cases can be substantial purely from fusion + staying in bf16 for I/O.\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## 4. Constraints (from CLAUDE.md / TASK.md)\n113\t\n114\t- **Triton is the primary implementation.** PyTorch only for metadata / launch\n115\t  plumbing (shape, dtype, output allocation, grid). **No** Torch compute fallback,\n116\t  **no** CPU/NumPy fallback, **no** CUDA-extension fallback, **no** alternate-impl\n117\t  fallback. A failing Triton kernel is invalid and must be fixed as Triton.\n118\t- **Immutable, sequential candidates** `c001, c002, …`; any meaningful source/config/\n119\t  launch change ⇒ new candidate ID; never reuse an ID or rewrite past records.\n120\t- **Evaluation only** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`; the five\n121\t  fixed workloads = one candidate evaluation. Budget: 100 evals; token soft limit\n122\t  1.0M / hard 1.2M. `final` is operator-approved only.\n123\t- **I cannot run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, Python, or the evaluator directly.**\n124\t  Local Bash is in \"don't ask\" / denied mode. ⇒ The *only* empirical signal is the\n125\t  feedback evaluation. First-try correctness matters a lot; wasted evals cost tokens.\n126\t- Skills `KernelWiki` (Blackwell/Hopper) and `ncu-report-skill` (B200/sm_100) are\n127\t  **not applicable** to this sm_80 task and are not used.\n128\t\n129\t---\n130\t\n131\t## 5. Numerical risks & mitigations\n132\t\n133\t| Risk | Cause | Mitigation |\n134\t|---|---|---|\n135\t| Wrong accumulation precision | Squaring/summing in bf16 loses ~8 mantissa bits; 4096-term sum drifts | Load bf16 → `.to(tl.float32)` **before** squaring; reduce with fp32 `tl.sum`. |\n136\t| Epsilon placement | Adding eps to RMS or to variance instead of to `E[x²]` | Compute `ms = sum(x²)/H`; `inv = rsqrt(ms + 1e-5)`. Match reference exactly. |\n137\t| Wrong denominator | Using `sum` not `mean`, or wrong H | Divide by `H = 4096` (constexpr). |\n138\t| Accidental centering | Copy-pasting a LayerNorm kernel | RMSNorm has **no** mean subtraction — do not subtract row mean. |\n139\t| Multiply-order drift | `x*w*inv_rms` vs `(x*inv_rms)*w` giving ULP diffs | Follow reference order `(x*inv)*w`; keep all fp32 until final cast. Differences are sub-bf16-ULP and safe, but match to be conservative. |\n140\t| Weight precision | Multiplying in bf16 | Upcast `weight` to fp32 before multiply (as reference does). |\n141\t| Final cast / rounding | Writing fp32 or wrong rounding | Cast result to bf16 (Triton default round-to-nearest matches torch) and store to a bf16 output tensor. |\n142\t| `rsqrt` accuracy | Fast-approx rsqrt could miss tolerance | Use fp32 `tl.rsqrt` (well within bf16 tolerance) or `1/tl.sqrt`; both are fp32-accurate enough. |\n143\t| Non-contiguous input | Evaluator hands a strided tensor | Pass row stride explicitly (`x.stride(0)`), or `.contiguous()` as a metadata-only safeguard. Prefer stride args to avoid a copy. |\n144\t| Overflow | `x²` overflow | bf16 range is huge; random inputs ⇒ no overflow. fp32 square is safe. |\n145\t| Div-by-zero / all-zero row | `ms = 0` | `+1e-5` guarantees a finite `inv_rms`. |\n146\t\n147\t**Tolerance note:** `definition.json` does not expose explicit `rtol/atol`. Because I\n148\tcannot see the checker thresholds and cannot test locally, the safe policy is to\n149\t**bit-for-bit mirror the reference math** (fp32 accumulate, fp32 weight, same eps\n150\tplacement, same op order, bf16 output). This maximizes the chance of passing whatever\n151\tbf16 tolerance the official evaluator uses.\n152\t\n153\t---\n154\t\n155\t## 6. Triton design space\n156\t\n157\tAll designs share the fused body: load a row of bf16, upcast fp32, square, fp32\n158\treduce, `rsqrt(mean+eps)`, scale, multiply fp32 weight, cast bf16, store.\n159\t\n160\t### A. One-program-per-row, single tile (baseline; classic FlashInfer/vLLM/tutorial form)\n161\t- `grid = (batch_size,)`; `BLOCK_SIZE = 4096` (= H, power of two → **no reduction mask**).\n162\t- Each program: `offs = tl.arange(0, 4096)`; load row, `xf=x.to(fp32)`; `s=tl.sum(xf*xf)`;\n163\t  `inv=tl.rsqrt(s/4096 + 1e-5)`; `y=(xf*inv)*w.to(fp32)`; store bf16.\n164\t- Weight loaded once per program (resident in L2 across programs → cheap).\n165\t- **Pros:** simplest, coalesced, one launch, bandwidth-optimal for large batch.\n166\t  **Cons:** low SM occupancy for tiny batch (but that regime is latency-bound anyway).\n167\t- **Tuning axes:** `num_warps ∈ {4, 8, 16}` (128/256/512 threads ⇒ 32/16/8 elems per\n168\t  thread), `num_stages`. This is the primary workhorse; likely `c001`.\n169\t\n170\t### B. Autotuned variant of A\n171\t- `triton.autotune` over `num_warps`/`num_stages` keyed on `batch_size` bucket, so the\n172\t  tiny and large regimes each get their best config. Risk: autotune runs configs at\n173\t  first call — must ensure the evaluator's timing excludes warmup, else noise. Treat as\n174\t  a follow-up candidate once a good static config is known.\n175\t\n176\t### C. Multiple rows per program (row blocking)\n177\t- Each program handles `R` rows (2D tile `[R, 4096]` or an inner loop), `grid = ceil(B/R)`.\n178\t- **Pro:** fewer, larger blocks → can improve memory-level parallelism and amortize\n179\t  per-program setup for the large cases; reduces grid size.\n180\t- **Con:** for tiny batch it *reduces* parallelism (fewer blocks); register/SRAM\n181\t  pressure grows with `R·4096`. Useful mainly as a large-batch tuning knob.\n182\t\n183\t### D. Split-reduction (split-K, two-pass) for the tiny cases\n184\t- Split H into `K` chunks; pass 1 writes `K` partial sums per row (grid `(B, K)`),\n185\t  pass 2 normalizes. For batch 7, `K=16` → 112 blocks → fills the machine.\n186\t- **Con:** adds a second kernel launch and a global round-trip. Since the tiny regime\n187\t  is *launch-latency* bound, a 2nd launch likely **hurts**, not helps. Keep as a\n188\t  contingency only if the tiny-batch speedup underperforms and profiling-by-inference\n189\t  says the reduction (not launch) is the bottleneck. **Low priority.**\n190\t\n191\t### E. Vectorization / access width\n192\t- Rows are contiguous (stride 1), so `tl.arange(0,4096)` loads are naturally coalesced;\n193\t  Triton vectorizes bf16 loads (up to 128-bit) automatically. Ensure the pointer math\n194\t  uses the row stride and unit inner stride. Little to gain beyond A if already\n195\t  coalesced, but verify no accidental masking/guard kills vectorization.\n196\t\n197\t### F. Grid-stride / persistent kernel\n198\t- One block per SM looping over rows. Marginal benefit here; grid of 14.5k blocks is\n199\t  already fine for A800 scheduling. Not worth the complexity unless launch-bound at\n200\t  large batch (unlikely). **Deprioritized.**\n201\t\n202\t**Chosen direction:** start with **A** (static, well-chosen `num_warps`), confirm\n203\tcorrectness + measure both regimes, then explore **B/C** as tuning refinements. Only\n204\tconsider **D** if the tiny regime clearly lags.\n205\t\n206\t---\n207\t\n208\t## 7. Entry point / integration contract\n209\t\n210\t`solution/solution.py` must expose `run(hidden_states, weight)` returning the bf16\n211\toutput tensor (mirroring the reference signature). Plumbing responsibilities (PyTorch\n212\tallowed here, metadata only):\n213\t\n214\t- Read `B, H = hidden_states.shape`; assert `H == 4096`.\n215\t- Allocate `out = torch.empty_like(hidden_states)` (bf16, same device).\n216\t- Pass `stride(0)` for the row stride (both x and out), unit inner stride.\n217\t- Compute `grid` from `B`; launch the Triton kernel; return `out`.\n218\t- Do **not** synchronize unnecessarily; let the evaluator handle timing/sync.\n219\t- Keep `EPS`, `H`, `BLOCK_SIZE` as constants/constexpr where possible.\n220\t\n221\tEdge cases to keep safe: `B` can be 7 (grid must be ≥1 and correct), and very large\n222\t(14509 — well within 1D grid limits). No masking needed on the hidden axis because\n223\t`BLOCK_SIZE == H == 4096`; batch axis uses `pid < B` implicitly via grid size.\n224\t\n225\t---\n226\t\n227\t## 8. Validation strategy (given no local execution)\n228\t\n229\tBecause the **only** empirical channel is `evaluate_candidate.sh feedback`, and every\n230\teval costs tokens, I will front-load correctness by construction:\n231\t\n232\t1. **Static review before each eval:** re-check dtype casts (bf16→fp32 before square),\n233\t   eps placement, denominator `H`, op order, bf16 store, stride/pointer math, grid,\n234\t   constexpr usage, and that no Torch-compute fallback path exists.\n235\t2. **Mirror reference math exactly** (Section 5) to de-risk the unseen tolerance.\n236\t3. **c001 = correctness anchor:** simplest correct design A with a sane `num_warps`\n237\t   (likely 8). Its purpose is to (a) confirm all five workloads PASS and (b) establish\n238\t   baseline speedups per regime. Record everything in `candidates.jsonl`.\n239\t4. **Optimize only after correctness is proven.** Change one variable per candidate\n240\t   (`num_warps`, then `num_stages`, then row-blocking, then autotune) so each eval\n241\t   yields an unambiguous signal. New ID per source change; never rewrite past records.\n242\t5. **Read the evaluator output per workload** (pass/fail + speedup), attribute wins to\n243\t   regimes (tiny vs large), and stop when the geomean converges or budget nears.\n244\t6. **Convergence / stop:** when successive candidates no longer improve geomean\n245\t   meaningfully (large case near the bandwidth roofline; tiny case near launch-latency\n246\t   floor), write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the rationale. Never run `final` without\n247\t   operator approval.\n248\t\n249\t**Interpreting results:** for the large cases, compare achieved time against the\n250\t~100–145 µs roofline (Section 3) to know how much headroom remains. For the tiny\n251\tcases, expect the fused single-launch kernel to already dominate the multi-launch\n252\treference; further kernel tuning yields little there, so don't overspend evals on it.\n253\t\n254\t---\n255\t\n256\t## 9. Risk register & open questions\n257\t\n258\t- **Unknown tolerance:** mitigated by exact-math mirroring; if a workload fails\n259\t  correctness unexpectedly, first suspect eps placement, denominator, or a stray bf16\n260\t  intermediate accumulation — not the tuning knobs.\n261\t- **Autotune warmup vs. timing:** unclear whether the evaluator isolates warmup; prefer\n262\t  a strong *static* config first, adopt autotune only if it demonstrably helps.\n263\t- **Occupancy vs. work for tiny batch:** likely launch-bound; avoid over-engineering\n264\t  split-K unless evidence says otherwise.\n265\t- **Bandwidth SKU (40 vs 80 GB):** changes the absolute roofline but not the strategy;\n266\t  the fused one-pass design is optimal regardless.\n267\t- **Contiguity assumption:** inputs are freshly generated randoms (very likely\n268\t  contiguous); still pass explicit strides so a strided input cannot silently break.\n269\t\n270\t---\n271\t\n272\t## 10. Direction summary (detail deferred to `docs/plan.md`)\n273\t\n274\t- **c001:** fused, one-pass, one-program-per-row Triton kernel, `BLOCK_SIZE=4096`,\n275\t  fp32 accumulate, exact reference math, `num_warps=8` — correctness anchor + baseline.\n276\t- **Then:** single-variable tuning — sweep `num_warps` (4/16) and `num_stages`;\n277\t  consider row-blocking (design C) and/or autotune (design B) for the large regime.\n278\t- **Contingency:** split-reduction (design D) only if the tiny regime underperforms.\n279\t- **Stop** at geomean convergence (large near roofline, tiny near launch floor) and\n280\t  write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`; `final` only with operator approval.\n281\t</content>\n282\t</invoke>\n283\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8588311-4b23-423c-adbc-8b67af18966a","uuid":"9f0387b1-f354-47cf-8282-3dda42536337","timestamp":"2026-09-16T16:55:46.033Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--flashinfer--rmsnorm_h4096/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — `rmsnorm_h4096` (FlashInfer, A800 / sm_80)\n\n## 0. Purpose & scope of this document\n\nThis is the analysis draft required before any code or executable plan. It captures:\nthe operation semantics, the exact numerics to reproduce, the workload regimes,\nhardware roofline, the Triton design space, numerical risks, and a validation\nstrategy given the tight feedback loop. No solution code and no `plan.md` are created\nhere. The concrete, ordered candidate steps belong in `docs/plan.md` (next turn).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation semantics\n\nRMSNorm (Root Mean Square Layer Normalization), row-wise over the hidden axis.\n\nReference (authoritative, from `task/definition.json`):\n\n```python\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef run(hidden_states, weight):\n    batch_size, hidden_size = hidden_states.shape      # hidden_size == 4096 (asserted)\n    EPS = 1e-5\n    x = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)                # upcast bf16 -> fp32\n    inv_rms = torch.rsqrt(x.pow(2).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + EPS)\n    y = (x * inv_rms) * weight.to(torch.float32)       # scale, then weight; fp32 math\n    return y.to(hidden_states.dtype)                   # downcast fp32 -> bf16\n```\n\nPer row `i` (length `H = 4096`):\n\n```\nms_i     = (1/H) * Σ_j x_ij^2            # mean of squares, fp32\ninv_rms_i = 1 / sqrt(ms_i + 1e-5)        # fp32\ny_ij     = (x_ij * inv_rms_i) * w_j      # fp32\nout_ij   = bf16(y_ij)\n```\n\nKey exact-semantics facts I must reproduce to stay inside tolerance:\n\n- **Accumulation dtype is fp32.** Input is bf16, upcast to fp32 *before* squaring.\n- **Reduction denominator is `H = 4096`** (a true mean, not sum). Epsilon `1e-5` is\n  added to the *mean of squares* (i.e. to `E[x^2]`), not to a Bessel-corrected\n  variance and not to the RMS itself.\n- **No mean-subtraction** — this is RMSNorm, not LayerNorm; there is no centering.\n- **Multiply order:** `(x * inv_rms) * weight`, all in fp32; `weight` is upcast to\n  fp32 first. Only the final result is cast back to bf16.\n- **Epsilon is a fixed constant `1e-5`** (also stated in the description). Hardcode it.\n\n### Shapes / dtypes\n- `hidden_states`: `[batch_size, 4096]`, **bf16**.\n- `weight`: `[4096]`, **bf16**.\n- `output`: `[batch_size, 4096]`, **bf16**.\n- `hidden_size` is a compile-time constant (`4096`, a power of two → nice for tiling,\n  no reduction-mask needed).\n- `batch_size` is the only variable axis.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workload analysis\n\nFive fixed feedback workloads (only `batch_size` varies):\n\n| uuid (short) | batch_size | rows | input bytes (bf16) | regime |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 9d40…5001 | 15 | 15 | 15·4096·2 ≈ 120 KB | tiny / latency-bound |\n| 1453… | 64 | 64 | ≈ 512 KB | small / latency-bound |\n| 33bf… | 7 | 7 | ≈ 57 KB | tiny / latency-bound |\n| 841b… | 14509 | 14509 | ≈ 113 MB | large / bandwidth-bound |\n| f0f5… | 14418 | 14418 | ≈ 113 MB | large / bandwidth-bound |\n\n**Two clearly distinct regimes**, and the geomean is over both, so both matter:\n\n- **Tiny/small (7, 15, 64 rows).** Far below the ~108-SM A800's ability to fill.\n  With \"one program per row\" the grid is 7–64 blocks → most SMs idle. But the total\n  work is minuscule (tens–hundreds of KB), so wall-time is dominated by **kernel-launch\n  latency and memory latency**, not throughput. The dominant win here is **fusion**:\n  the torch reference launches many kernels and materializes fp32 intermediates,\n  whereas a single fused Triton kernel is one launch reading bf16 once / writing once.\n- **Large (14418, 14509 rows).** ~113 MB read + ~113 MB written ⇒ ~226 MB of HBM\n  traffic (weight is 8 KB, cached, negligible). This regime is strictly\n  **memory-bandwidth-bound**; the objective is to hit the bf16-in/bf16-out roofline\n  and avoid the reference's extra fp32-intermediate traffic.\n\n**Implication:** a single, lean, fused, one-pass kernel should win in *both* regimes\nagainst the un-fused reference. The optimization headroom is (a) not leaving bandwidth\non the table for the large cases and (b) minimizing overhead for the tiny cases.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hardware roofline (A800, sm_80)\n\n- A800 = GA100 (108 SMs), sm_80, HBM2e. Peak HBM bandwidth ≈ **1.5–2.0 TB/s**\n  (≈2.0 TB/s for the 80 GB SKU, ≈1.55 TB/s for the 40 GB SKU); 40 MB L2.\n- Arithmetic intensity of RMSNorm is very low: ~2 FLOP-ish per element for the\n  square + a couple of multiplies, against 2 bytes read + 2 bytes written per element.\n  → **Memory bound.** FP32 ALU throughput is irrelevant at these sizes.\n\n**Ideal large-case time** (bf16 in + bf16 out, weight negligible):\n`226 MB / 1.8 TB/s ≈ 125 µs` (≈100 µs at 2.0 TB/s, ≈145 µs at 1.55 TB/s).\nThis is the target floor for batch≈14.5k. Any fused kernel that reads each input\nelement once and writes each output once, with coalesced accesses, should approach it.\n\n**Reference inefficiency:** the torch reference upcasts to fp32 (`x = …to(float32)`\nis a 4096·B·4-byte materialization = 2× the input traffic at fp32), then `x.pow(2)`,\nthen `.mean(...)`, then broadcast-mul twice, then downcast — multiple passes over\nlarge fp32 buffers plus several kernel launches. So the *speedup vs reference* on the\nlarge cases can be substantial purely from fusion + staying in bf16 for I/O.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Constraints (from CLAUDE.md / TASK.md)\n\n- **Triton is the primary implementation.** PyTorch only for metadata / launch\n  plumbing (shape, dtype, output allocation, grid). **No** Torch compute fallback,\n  **no** CPU/NumPy fallback, **no** CUDA-extension fallback, **no** alternate-impl\n  fallback. A failing Triton kernel is invalid and must be fixed as Triton.\n- **Immutable, sequential candidates** `c001, c002, …`; any meaningful source/config/\n  launch change ⇒ new candidate ID; never reuse an ID or rewrite past records.\n- **Evaluation only** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`; the five\n  fixed workloads = one candidate evaluation. Budget: 100 evals; token soft limit\n  1.0M / hard 1.2M. `final` is operator-approved only.\n- **I cannot run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, Python, or the evaluator directly.**\n  Local Bash is in \"don't ask\" / denied mode. ⇒ The *only* empirical signal is the\n  feedback evaluation. First-try correctness matters a lot; wasted evals cost tokens.\n- Skills `KernelWiki` (Blackwell/Hopper) and `ncu-report-skill` (B200/sm_100) are\n  **not applicable** to this sm_80 task and are not used.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Numerical risks & mitigations\n\n| Risk | Cause | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wrong accumulation precision | Squaring/summing in bf16 loses ~8 mantissa bits; 4096-term sum drifts | Load bf16 → `.to(tl.float32)` **before** squaring; reduce with fp32 `tl.sum`. |\n| Epsilon placement | Adding eps to RMS or to variance instead of to `E[x²]` | Compute `ms = sum(x²)/H`; `inv = rsqrt(ms + 1e-5)`. Match reference exactly. |\n| Wrong denominator | Using `sum` not `mean`, or wrong H | Divide by `H = 4096` (constexpr). |\n| Accidental centering | Copy-pasting a LayerNorm kernel | RMSNorm has **no** mean subtraction — do not subtract row mean. |\n| Multiply-order drift | `x*w*inv_rms` vs `(x*inv_rms)*w` giving ULP diffs | Follow reference order `(x*inv)*w`; keep all fp32 until final cast. Differences are sub-bf16-ULP and safe, but match to be conservative. |\n| Weight precision | Multiplying in bf16 | Upcast `weight` to fp32 before multiply (as reference does). |\n| Final cast / rounding | Writing fp32 or wrong rounding | Cast result to bf16 (Triton default round-to-nearest matches torch) and store to a bf16 output tensor. |\n| `rsqrt` accuracy | Fast-approx rsqrt could miss tolerance | Use fp32 `tl.rsqrt` (well within bf16 tolerance) or `1/tl.sqrt`; both are fp32-accurate enough. |\n| Non-contiguous input | Evaluator hands a strided tensor | Pass row stride explicitly (`x.stride(0)`), or `.contiguous()` as a metadata-only safeguard. Prefer stride args to avoid a copy. |\n| Overflow | `x²` overflow | bf16 range is huge; random inputs ⇒ no overflow. fp32 square is safe. |\n| Div-by-zero / all-zero row | `ms = 0` | `+1e-5` guarantees a finite `inv_rms`. |\n\n**Tolerance note:** `definition.json` does not expose explicit `rtol/atol`. Because I\ncannot see the checker thresholds and cannot test locally, the safe policy is to\n**bit-for-bit mirror the reference math** (fp32 accumulate, fp32 weight, same eps\nplacement, same op order, bf16 output). This maximizes the chance of passing whatever\nbf16 tolerance the official evaluator uses.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Triton design space\n\nAll designs share the fused body: load a row of bf16, upcast fp32, square, fp32\nreduce, `rsqrt(mean+eps)`, scale, multiply fp32 weight, cast bf16, store.\n\n### A. One-program-per-row, single tile (baseline; classic FlashInfer/vLLM/tutorial form)\n- `grid = (batch_size,)`; `BLOCK_SIZE = 4096` (= H, power of two → **no reduction mask**).\n- Each program: `offs = tl.arange(0, 4096)`; load row, `xf=x.to(fp32)`; `s=tl.sum(xf*xf)`;\n  `inv=tl.rsqrt(s/4096 + 1e-5)`; `y=(xf*inv)*w.to(fp32)`; store bf16.\n- Weight loaded once per program (resident in L2 across programs → cheap).\n- **Pros:** simplest, coalesced, one launch, bandwidth-optimal for large batch.\n  **Cons:** low SM occupancy for tiny batch (but that regime is latency-bound anyway).\n- **Tuning axes:** `num_warps ∈ {4, 8, 16}` (128/256/512 threads ⇒ 32/16/8 elems per\n  thread), `num_stages`. This is the primary workhorse; likely `c001`.\n\n### B. Autotuned variant of A\n- `triton.autotune` over `num_warps`/`num_stages` keyed on `batch_size` bucket, so the\n  tiny and large regimes each get their best config. Risk: autotune runs configs at\n  first call — must ensure the evaluator's timing excludes warmup, else noise. Treat as\n  a follow-up candidate once a good static config is known.\n\n### C. Multiple rows per program (row blocking)\n- Each program handles `R` rows (2D tile `[R, 4096]` or an inner loop), `grid = ceil(B/R)`.\n- **Pro:** fewer, larger blocks → can improve memory-level parallelism and amortize\n  per-program setup for the large cases; reduces grid size.\n- **Con:** for tiny batch it *reduces* parallelism (fewer blocks); register/SRAM\n  pressure grows with `R·4096`. Useful mainly as a large-batch tuning knob.\n\n### D. Split-reduction (split-K, two-pass) for the tiny cases\n- Split H into `K` chunks; pass 1 writes `K` partial sums per row (grid `(B, K)`),\n  pass 2 normalizes. For batch 7, `K=16` → 112 blocks → fills the machine.\n- **Con:** adds a second kernel launch and a global round-trip. Since the tiny regime\n  is *launch-latency* bound, a 2nd launch likely **hurts**, not helps. Keep as a\n  contingency only if the tiny-batch speedup underperforms and profiling-by-inference\n  says the reduction (not launch) is the bottleneck. **Low priority.**\n\n### E. Vectorization / access width\n- Rows are contiguous (stride 1), so `tl.arange(0,4096)` loads are naturally coalesced;\n  Triton vectorizes bf16 loads (up to 128-bit) automatically. Ensure the pointer math\n  uses the row stride and unit inner stride. Little to gain beyond A if already\n  coalesced, but verify no accidental masking/guard kills vectorization.\n\n### F. Grid-stride / persistent kernel\n- One block per SM looping over rows. Marginal benefit here; grid of 14.5k blocks is\n  already fine for A800 scheduling. Not worth the complexity unless launch-bound at\n  large batch (unlikely). **Deprioritized.**\n\n**Chosen direction:** start with **A** (static, well-chosen `num_warps`), confirm\ncorrectness + measure both regimes, then explore **B/C** as tuning refinements. Only\nconsider **D** if the tiny regime clearly lags.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Entry point / integration contract\n\n`solution/solution.py` must expose `run(hidden_states, weight)` returning the bf16\noutput tensor (mirroring the reference signature). Plumbing responsibilities (PyTorch\nallowed here, metadata only):\n\n- Read `B, H = hidden_states.shape`; assert `H == 4096`.\n- Allocate `out = torch.empty_like(hidden_states)` (bf16, same device).\n- Pass `stride(0)` for the row stride (both x and out), unit inner stride.\n- Compute `grid` from `B`; launch the Triton kernel; return `out`.\n- Do **not** synchronize unnecessarily; let the evaluator handle timing/sync.\n- Keep `EPS`, `H`, `BLOCK_SIZE` as constants/constexpr where possible.\n\nEdge cases to keep safe: `B` can be 7 (grid must be ≥1 and correct), and very large\n(14509 — well within 1D grid limits). No masking needed on the hidden axis because\n`BLOCK_SIZE == H == 4096`; batch axis uses `pid < B` implicitly via grid size.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Validation strategy (given no local execution)\n\nBecause the **only** empirical channel is `evaluate_candidate.sh feedback`, and every\neval costs tokens, I will front-load correctness by construction:\n\n1. **Static review before each eval:** re-check dtype casts (bf16→fp32 before square),\n   eps placement, denominator `H`, op order, bf16 store, stride/pointer math, grid,\n   constexpr usage, and that no Torch-compute fallback path exists.\n2. **Mirror reference math exactly** (Section 5) to de-risk the unseen tolerance.\n3. **c001 = correctness anchor:** simplest correct design A with a sane `num_warps`\n   (likely 8). Its purpose is to (a) confirm all five workloads PASS and (b) establish\n   baseline speedups per regime. Record everything in `candidates.jsonl`.\n4. **Optimize only after correctness is proven.** Change one variable per candidate\n   (`num_warps`, then `num_stages`, then row-blocking, then autotune) so each eval\n   yields an unambiguous signal. New ID per source change; never rewrite past records.\n5. **Read the evaluator output per workload** (pass/fail + speedup), attribute wins to\n   regimes (tiny vs large), and stop when the geomean converges or budget nears.\n6. **Convergence / stop:** when successive candidates no longer improve geomean\n   meaningfully (large case near the bandwidth roofline; tiny case near launch-latency\n   floor), write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the rationale. Never run `final` without\n   operator approval.\n\n**Interpreting results:** for the large cases, compare achieved time against the\n~100–145 µs roofline (Section 3) to know how much headroom remains. For the tiny\ncases, expect the fused single-launch kernel to already dominate the multi-launch\nreference; further kernel tuning yields little there, so don't overspend evals on it.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Risk register & open questions\n\n- **Unknown tolerance:** mitigated by exact-math mirroring; if a workload fails\n  correctness unexpectedly, first suspect eps placement, denominator, or a stray bf16\n  intermediate accumulation — not the tuning knobs.\n- **Autotune warmup vs. timing:** unclear whether the evaluator isolates warmup; prefer\n  a strong *static* config first, adopt autotune only if it demonstrably helps.\n- **Occupancy vs. work for tiny batch:** likely launch-bound; avoid over-engineering\n  split-K unless evidence says otherwise.\n- **Bandwidth SKU (40 vs 80 GB):** changes the absolute roofline but not the strategy;\n  the fused one-pass design is optimal regardless.\n- **Contiguity assumption:** inputs are freshly generated randoms (very likely\n  contiguous); still pass explicit strides so a strided input cannot silently break.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Direction summary (detail deferred to `docs/plan.md`)\n\n- **c001:** fused, one-pass, one-program-per-row Triton kernel, `BLOCK_SIZE=4096`,\n  fp32 accumulate, exact reference math, `num_warps=8` — correctness anchor + baseline.\n- **Then:** single-variable tuning — sweep `num_warps` (4/16) and `num_stages`;\n  consider row-blocking (design C) and/or autotune (design B) for the large regime.\n- **Contingency:** split-reduction (design D) only if the tiny regime underperforms.\n- **Stop** at geomean convergence (large near roofline, tiny near launch floor) and\n  write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`; `final` only with operator approval.\n</content>\n</invoke>\n","numLines":283,"startLine":1,"totalLines":283}}}
