{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ubn2RwAFwQg7Hd5V9LE54R","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — `rmsnorm_h4096` (FlashInfer, A800 / sm_80)\n2\t\n3\t## 0. Purpose & scope of this document\n4\t\n5\tThis is the analysis draft required before any code or executable plan. It captures:\n6\tthe operation semantics, the exact numerics to reproduce, the workload regimes,\n7\thardware roofline, the Triton design space, numerical risks, and a validation\n8\tstrategy given the tight feedback loop. No solution code and no `plan.md` are created\n9\there. The concrete, ordered candidate steps belong in `docs/plan.md` (next turn).\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## 1. Operation semantics\n14\t\n15\tRMSNorm (Root Mean Square Layer Normalization), row-wise over the hidden axis.\n16\t\n17\tReference (authoritative, from `task/definition.json`):\n18\t\n19\t```python\n20\t@torch.no_grad()\n21\tdef run(hidden_states, weight):\n22\t    batch_size, hidden_size = hidden_states.shape      # hidden_size == 4096 (asserted)\n23\t    EPS = 1e-5\n24\t    x = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)                # upcast bf16 -> fp32\n25\t    inv_rms = torch.rsqrt(x.pow(2).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + EPS)\n26\t    y = (x * inv_rms) * weight.to(torch.float32)       # scale, then weight; fp32 math\n27\t    return y.to(hidden_states.dtype)                   # downcast fp32 -> bf16\n28\t```\n29\t\n30\tPer row `i` (length `H = 4096`):\n31\t\n32\t```\n33\tms_i     = (1/H) * Σ_j x_ij^2            # mean of squares, fp32\n34\tinv_rms_i = 1 / sqrt(ms_i + 1e-5)        # fp32\n35\ty_ij     = (x_ij * inv_rms_i) * w_j      # fp32\n36\tout_ij   = bf16(y_ij)\n37\t```\n38\t\n39\tKey exact-semantics facts I must reproduce to stay inside tolerance:\n40\t\n41\t- **Accumulation dtype is fp32.** Input is bf16, upcast to fp32 *before* squaring.\n42\t- **Reduction denominator is `H = 4096`** (a true mean, not sum). Epsilon `1e-5` is\n43\t  added to the *mean of squares* (i.e. to `E[x^2]`), not to a Bessel-corrected\n44\t  variance and not to the RMS itself.\n45\t- **No mean-subtraction** — this is RMSNorm, not LayerNorm; there is no centering.\n46\t- **Multiply order:** `(x * inv_rms) * weight`, all in fp32; `weight` is upcast to\n47\t  fp32 first. Only the final result is cast back to bf16.\n48\t- **Epsilon is a fixed constant `1e-5`** (also stated in the description). Hardcode it.\n49\t\n50\t### Shapes / dtypes\n51\t- `hidden_states`: `[batch_size, 4096]`, **bf16**.\n52\t- `weight`: `[4096]`, **bf16**.\n53\t- `output`: `[batch_size, 4096]`, **bf16**.\n54\t- `hidden_size` is a compile-time constant (`4096`, a power of two → nice for tiling,\n55\t  no reduction-mask needed).\n56\t- `batch_size` is the only variable axis.\n57\t\n58\t---\n59\t\n60\t## 2. Workload analysis\n61\t\n62\tFive fixed feedback workloads (only `batch_size` varies):\n63\t\n64\t| uuid (short) | batch_size | rows | input bytes (bf16) | regime |\n65\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n66\t| 9d40…5001 | 15 | 15 | 15·4096·2 ≈ 120 KB | tiny / latency-bound |\n67\t| 1453… | 64 | 64 | ≈ 512 KB | small / latency-bound |\n68\t| 33bf… | 7 | 7 | ≈ 57 KB | tiny / latency-bound |\n69\t| 841b… | 14509 | 14509 | ≈ 113 MB | large / bandwidth-bound |\n70\t| f0f5… | 14418 | 14418 | ≈ 113 MB | large / bandwidth-bound |\n71\t\n72\t**Two clearly distinct regimes**, and the geomean is over both, so both matter:\n73\t\n74\t- **Tiny/small (7, 15, 64 rows).** Far below the ~108-SM A800's ability to fill.\n75\t  With \"one program per row\" the grid is 7–64 blocks → most SMs idle. But the total\n76\t  work is minuscule (tens–hundreds of KB), so wall-time is dominated by **kernel-launch\n77\t  latency and memory latency**, not throughput. The dominant win here is **fusion**:\n78\t  the torch reference launches many kernels and materializes fp32 intermediates,\n79\t  whereas a single fused Triton kernel is one launch reading bf16 once / writing once.\n80\t- **Large (14418, 14509 rows).** ~113 MB read + ~113 MB written ⇒ ~226 MB of HBM\n81\t  traffic (weight is 8 KB, cached, negligible). This regime is strictly\n82\t  **memory-bandwidth-bound**; the objective is to hit the bf16-in/bf16-out roofline\n83\t  and avoid the reference's extra fp32-intermediate traffic.\n84\t\n85\t**Implication:** a single, lean, fused, one-pass kernel should win in *both* regimes\n86\tagainst the un-fused reference. The optimization headroom is (a) not leaving bandwidth\n87\ton the table for the large cases and (b) minimizing overhead for the tiny cases.\n88\t\n89\t---\n90\t\n91\t## 3. Hardware roofline (A800, sm_80)\n92\t\n93\t- A800 = GA100 (108 SMs), sm_80, HBM2e. Peak HBM bandwidth ≈ **1.5–2.0 TB/s**\n94\t  (≈2.0 TB/s for the 80 GB SKU, ≈1.55 TB/s for the 40 GB SKU); 40 MB L2.\n95\t- Arithmetic intensity of RMSNorm is very low: ~2 FLOP-ish per element for the\n96\t  square + a couple of multiplies, against 2 bytes read + 2 bytes written per element.\n97\t  → **Memory bound.** FP32 ALU throughput is irrelevant at these sizes.\n98\t\n99\t**Ideal large-case time** (bf16 in + bf16 out, weight negligible):\n100\t`226 MB / 1.8 TB/s ≈ 125 µs` (≈100 µs at 2.0 TB/s, ≈145 µs at 1.55 TB/s).\n101\tThis is the target floor for batch≈14.5k. Any fused kernel that reads each input\n102\telement once and writes each output once, with coalesced accesses, should approach it.\n103\t\n104\t**Reference inefficiency:** the torch reference upcasts to fp32 (`x = …to(float32)`\n105\tis a 4096·B·4-byte materialization = 2× the input traffic at fp32), then `x.pow(2)`,\n106\tthen `.mean(...)`, then broadcast-mul twice, then downcast — multiple passes over\n107\tlarge fp32 buffers plus several kernel launches. So the *speedup vs reference* on the\n108\tlarge cases can be substantial purely from fusion + staying in bf16 for I/O.\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## 4. Constraints (from CLAUDE.md / TASK.md)\n113\t\n114\t- **Triton is the primary implementation.** PyTorch only for metadata / launch\n115\t  plumbing (shape, dtype, output allocation, grid). **No** Torch compute fallback,\n116\t  **no** CPU/NumPy fallback, **no** CUDA-extension fallback, **no** alternate-impl\n117\t  fallback. A failing Triton kernel is invalid and must be fixed as Triton.\n118\t- **Immutable, sequential candidates** `c001, c002, …`; any meaningful source/config/\n119\t  launch change ⇒ new candidate ID; never reuse an ID or rewrite past records.\n120\t- **Evaluation only** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`; the five\n121\t  fixed workloads = one candidate evaluation. Budget: 100 evals; token soft limit\n122\t  1.0M / hard 1.2M. `final` is operator-approved only.\n123\t- **I cannot run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, Python, or the evaluator directly.**\n124\t  Local Bash is in \"don't ask\" / denied mode. ⇒ The *only* empirical signal is the\n125\t  feedback evaluation. First-try correctness matters a lot; wasted evals cost tokens.\n126\t- Skills `KernelWiki` (Blackwell/Hopper) and `ncu-report-skill` (B200/sm_100) are\n127\t  **not applicable** to this sm_80 task and are not used.\n128\t\n129\t---\n130\t\n131\t## 5. Numerical risks & mitigations\n132\t\n133\t| Risk | Cause | Mitigation |\n134\t|---|---|---|\n135\t| Wrong accumulation precision | Squaring/summing in bf16 loses ~8 mantissa bits; 4096-term sum drifts | Load bf16 → `.to(tl.float32)` **before** squaring; reduce with fp32 `tl.sum`. |\n136\t| Epsilon placement | Adding eps to RMS or to variance instead of to `E[x²]` | Compute `ms = sum(x²)/H`; `inv = rsqrt(ms + 1e-5)`. Match reference exactly. |\n137\t| Wrong denominator | Using `sum` not `mean`, or wrong H | Divide by `H = 4096` (constexpr). |\n138\t| Accidental centering | Copy-pasting a LayerNorm kernel | RMSNorm has **no** mean subtraction — do not subtract row mean. |\n139\t| Multiply-order drift | `x*w*inv_rms` vs `(x*inv_rms)*w` giving ULP diffs | Follow reference order `(x*inv)*w`; keep all fp32 until final cast. Differences are sub-bf16-ULP and safe, but match to be conservative. |\n140\t| Weight precision | Multiplying in bf16 | Upcast `weight` to fp32 before multiply (as reference does). |\n141\t| Final cast / rounding | Writing fp32 or wrong rounding | Cast result to bf16 (Triton default round-to-nearest matches torch) and store to a bf16 output tensor. |\n142\t| `rsqrt` accuracy | Fast-approx rsqrt could miss tolerance | Use fp32 `tl.rsqrt` (well within bf16 tolerance) or `1/tl.sqrt`; both are fp32-accurate enough. |\n143\t| Non-contiguous input | Evaluator hands a strided tensor | Pass row stride explicitly (`x.stride(0)`), or `.contiguous()` as a metadata-only safeguard. Prefer stride args to avoid a copy. |\n144\t| Overflow | `x²` overflow | bf16 range is huge; random inputs ⇒ no overflow. fp32 square is safe. |\n145\t| Div-by-zero / all-zero row | `ms = 0` | `+1e-5` guarantees a finite `inv_rms`. |\n146\t\n147\t**Tolerance note:** `definition.json` does not expose explicit `rtol/atol`. Because I\n148\tcannot see the checker thresholds and cannot test locally, the safe policy is to\n149\t**bit-for-bit mirror the reference math** (fp32 accumulate, fp32 weight, same eps\n150\tplacement, same op order, bf16 output). This maximizes the chance of passing whatever\n151\tbf16 tolerance the official evaluator uses.\n152\t\n153\t---\n154\t\n155\t## 6. Triton design space\n156\t\n157\tAll designs share the fused body: load a row of bf16, upcast fp32, square, fp32\n158\treduce, `rsqrt(mean+eps)`, scale, multiply fp32 weight, cast bf16, store.\n159\t\n160\t### A. One-program-per-row, single tile (baseline; classic FlashInfer/vLLM/tutorial form)\n161\t- `grid = (batch_size,)`; `BLOCK_SIZE = 4096` (= H, power of two → **no reduction mask**).\n162\t- Each program: `offs = tl.arange(0, 4096)`; load row, `xf=x.to(fp32)`; `s=tl.sum(xf*xf)`;\n163\t  `inv=tl.rsqrt(s/4096 + 1e-5)`; `y=(xf*inv)*w.to(fp32)`; store bf16.\n164\t- Weight loaded once per program (resident in L2 across programs → cheap).\n165\t- **Pros:** simplest, coalesced, one launch, bandwidth-optimal for large batch.\n166\t  **Cons:** low SM occupancy for tiny batch (but that regime is latency-bound anyway).\n167\t- **Tuning axes:** `num_warps ∈ {4, 8, 16}` (128/256/512 threads ⇒ 32/16/8 elems per\n168\t  thread), `num_stages`. This is the primary workhorse; likely `c001`.\n169\t\n170\t### B. Autotuned variant of A\n171\t- `triton.autotune` over `num_warps`/`num_stages` keyed on `batch_size` bucket, so the\n172\t  tiny and large regimes each get their best config. Risk: autotune runs configs at\n173\t  first call — must ensure the evaluator's timing excludes warmup, else noise. Treat as\n174\t  a follow-up candidate once a good static config is known.\n175\t\n176\t### C. Multiple rows per program (row blocking)\n177\t- Each program handles `R` rows (2D tile `[R, 4096]` or an inner loop), `grid = ceil(B/R)`.\n178\t- **Pro:** fewer, larger blocks → can improve memory-level parallelism and amortize\n179\t  per-program setup for the large cases; reduces grid size.\n180\t- **Con:** for tiny batch it *reduces* parallelism (fewer blocks); register/SRAM\n181\t  pressure grows with `R·4096`. Useful mainly as a large-batch tuning knob.\n182\t\n183\t### D. Split-reduction (split-K, two-pass) for the tiny cases\n184\t- Split H into `K` chunks; pass 1 writes `K` partial sums per row (grid `(B, K)`),\n185\t  pass 2 normalizes. For batch 7, `K=16` → 112 blocks → fills the machine.\n186\t- **Con:** adds a second kernel launch and a global round-trip. Since the tiny regime\n187\t  is *launch-latency* bound, a 2nd launch likely **hurts**, not helps. Keep as a\n188\t  contingency only if the tiny-batch speedup underperforms and profiling-by-inference\n189\t  says the reduction (not launch) is the bottleneck. **Low priority.**\n190\t\n191\t### E. Vectorization / access width\n192\t- Rows are contiguous (stride 1), so `tl.arange(0,4096)` loads are naturally coalesced;\n193\t  Triton vectorizes bf16 loads (up to 128-bit) automatically. Ensure the pointer math\n194\t  uses the row stride and unit inner stride. Little to gain beyond A if already\n195\t  coalesced, but verify no accidental masking/guard kills vectorization.\n196\t\n197\t### F. Grid-stride / persistent kernel\n198\t- One block per SM looping over rows. Marginal benefit here; grid of 14.5k blocks is\n199\t  already fine for A800 scheduling. Not worth the complexity unless launch-bound at\n200\t  large batch (unlikely). **Deprioritized.**\n201\t\n202\t**Chosen direction:** start with **A** (static, well-chosen `num_warps`), confirm\n203\tcorrectness + measure both regimes, then explore **B/C** as tuning refinements. Only\n204\tconsider **D** if the tiny regime clearly lags.\n205\t\n206\t---\n207\t\n208\t## 7. Entry point / integration contract\n209\t\n210\t`solution/solution.py` must expose `run(hidden_states, weight)` returning the bf16\n211\toutput tensor (mirroring the reference signature). Plumbing responsibilities (PyTorch\n212\tallowed here, metadata only):\n213\t\n214\t- Read `B, H = hidden_states.shape`; assert `H == 4096`.\n215\t- Allocate `out = torch.empty_like(hidden_states)` (bf16, same device).\n216\t- Pass `stride(0)` for the row stride (both x and out), unit inner stride.\n217\t- Compute `grid` from `B`; launch the Triton kernel; return `out`.\n218\t- Do **not** synchronize unnecessarily; let the evaluator handle timing/sync.\n219\t- Keep `EPS`, `H`, `BLOCK_SIZE` as constants/constexpr where possible.\n220\t\n221\tEdge cases to keep safe: `B` can be 7 (grid must be ≥1 and correct), and very large\n222\t(14509 — well within 1D grid limits). No masking needed on the hidden axis because\n223\t`BLOCK_SIZE == H == 4096`; batch axis uses `pid < B` implicitly via grid size.\n224\t\n225\t---\n226\t\n227\t## 8. Validation strategy (given no local execution)\n228\t\n229\tBecause the **only** empirical channel is `evaluate_candidate.sh feedback`, and every\n230\teval costs tokens, I will front-load correctness by construction:\n231\t\n232\t1. **Static review before each eval:** re-check dtype casts (bf16→fp32 before square),\n233\t   eps placement, denominator `H`, op order, bf16 store, stride/pointer math, grid,\n234\t   constexpr usage, and that no Torch-compute fallback path exists.\n235\t2. **Mirror reference math exactly** (Section 5) to de-risk the unseen tolerance.\n236\t3. **c001 = correctness anchor:** simplest correct design A with a sane `num_warps`\n237\t   (likely 8). Its purpose is to (a) confirm all five workloads PASS and (b) establish\n238\t   baseline speedups per regime. Record everything in `candidates.jsonl`.\n239\t4. **Optimize only after correctness is proven.** Change one variable per candidate\n240\t   (`num_warps`, then `num_stages`, then row-blocking, then autotune) so each eval\n241\t   yields an unambiguous signal. New ID per source change; never rewrite past records.\n242\t5. **Read the evaluator output per workload** (pass/fail + speedup), attribute wins to\n243\t   regimes (tiny vs large), and stop when the geomean converges or budget nears.\n244\t6. **Convergence / stop:** when successive candidates no longer improve geomean\n245\t   meaningfully (large case near the bandwidth roofline; tiny case near launch-latency\n246\t   floor), write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the rationale. Never run `final` without\n247\t   operator approval.\n248\t\n249\t**Interpreting results:** for the large cases, compare achieved time against the\n250\t~100–145 µs roofline (Section 3) to know how much headroom remains. For the tiny\n251\tcases, expect the fused single-launch kernel to already dominate the multi-launch\n252\treference; further kernel tuning yields little there, so don't overspend evals on it.\n253\t\n254\t---\n255\t\n256\t## 9. Risk register & open questions\n257\t\n258\t- **Unknown tolerance:** mitigated by exact-math mirroring; if a workload fails\n259\t  correctness unexpectedly, first suspect eps placement, denominator, or a stray bf16\n260\t  intermediate accumulation — not the tuning knobs.\n261\t- **Autotune warmup vs. timing:** unclear whether the evaluator isolates warmup; prefer\n262\t  a strong *static* config first, adopt autotune only if it demonstrably helps.\n263\t- **Occupancy vs. work for tiny batch:** likely launch-bound; avoid over-engineering\n264\t  split-K unless evidence says otherwise.\n265\t- **Bandwidth SKU (40 vs 80 GB):** changes the absolute roofline but not the strategy;\n266\t  the fused one-pass design is optimal regardless.\n267\t- **Contiguity assumption:** inputs are freshly generated randoms (very likely\n268\t  contiguous); still pass explicit strides so a strided input cannot silently break.\n269\t\n270\t---\n271\t\n272\t## 10. Direction summary (detail deferred to `docs/plan.md`)\n273\t\n274\t- **c001:** fused, one-pass, one-program-per-row Triton kernel, `BLOCK_SIZE=4096`,\n275\t  fp32 accumulate, exact reference math, `num_warps=8` — correctness anchor + baseline.\n276\t- **Then:** single-variable tuning — sweep `num_warps` (4/16) and `num_stages`;\n277\t  consider row-blocking (design C) and/or autotune (design B) for the large regime.\n278\t- **Contingency:** split-reduction (design D) only if the tiny regime underperforms.\n279\t- **Stop** at geomean convergence (large near roofline, tiny near launch floor) and\n280\t  write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`; `final` only with operator approval.\n281\t</content>\n282\t</invoke>\n283\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d241f2d5-b247-435b-b5e7-bf68463d035a","uuid":"57962126-7107-4c30-bbbd-5528fb75d879","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:20:37.931Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--flashinfer--rmsnorm_h4096/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — `rmsnorm_h4096` (FlashInfer, A800 / sm_80)\n\n## 0. Purpose & scope of this document\n\nThis is the analysis draft required before any code or executable plan. It captures:\nthe operation semantics, the exact numerics to reproduce, the workload regimes,\nhardware roofline, the Triton design space, numerical risks, and a validation\nstrategy given the tight feedback loop. No solution code and no `plan.md` are created\nhere. The concrete, ordered candidate steps belong in `docs/plan.md` (next turn).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation semantics\n\nRMSNorm (Root Mean Square Layer Normalization), row-wise over the hidden axis.\n\nReference (authoritative, from `task/definition.json`):\n\n```python\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef run(hidden_states, weight):\n    batch_size, hidden_size = hidden_states.shape      # hidden_size == 4096 (asserted)\n    EPS = 1e-5\n    x = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)                # upcast bf16 -> fp32\n    inv_rms = torch.rsqrt(x.pow(2).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + EPS)\n    y = (x * inv_rms) * weight.to(torch.float32)       # scale, then weight; fp32 math\n    return y.to(hidden_states.dtype)                   # downcast fp32 -> bf16\n```\n\nPer row `i` (length `H = 4096`):\n\n```\nms_i     = (1/H) * Σ_j x_ij^2            # mean of squares, fp32\ninv_rms_i = 1 / sqrt(ms_i + 1e-5)        # fp32\ny_ij     = (x_ij * inv_rms_i) * w_j      # fp32\nout_ij   = bf16(y_ij)\n```\n\nKey exact-semantics facts I must reproduce to stay inside tolerance:\n\n- **Accumulation dtype is fp32.** Input is bf16, upcast to fp32 *before* squaring.\n- **Reduction denominator is `H = 4096`** (a true mean, not sum). Epsilon `1e-5` is\n  added to the *mean of squares* (i.e. to `E[x^2]`), not to a Bessel-corrected\n  variance and not to the RMS itself.\n- **No mean-subtraction** — this is RMSNorm, not LayerNorm; there is no centering.\n- **Multiply order:** `(x * inv_rms) * weight`, all in fp32; `weight` is upcast to\n  fp32 first. Only the final result is cast back to bf16.\n- **Epsilon is a fixed constant `1e-5`** (also stated in the description). Hardcode it.\n\n### Shapes / dtypes\n- `hidden_states`: `[batch_size, 4096]`, **bf16**.\n- `weight`: `[4096]`, **bf16**.\n- `output`: `[batch_size, 4096]`, **bf16**.\n- `hidden_size` is a compile-time constant (`4096`, a power of two → nice for tiling,\n  no reduction-mask needed).\n- `batch_size` is the only variable axis.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workload analysis\n\nFive fixed feedback workloads (only `batch_size` varies):\n\n| uuid (short) | batch_size | rows | input bytes (bf16) | regime |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 9d40…5001 | 15 | 15 | 15·4096·2 ≈ 120 KB | tiny / latency-bound |\n| 1453… | 64 | 64 | ≈ 512 KB | small / latency-bound |\n| 33bf… | 7 | 7 | ≈ 57 KB | tiny / latency-bound |\n| 841b… | 14509 | 14509 | ≈ 113 MB | large / bandwidth-bound |\n| f0f5… | 14418 | 14418 | ≈ 113 MB | large / bandwidth-bound |\n\n**Two clearly distinct regimes**, and the geomean is over both, so both matter:\n\n- **Tiny/small (7, 15, 64 rows).** Far below the ~108-SM A800's ability to fill.\n  With \"one program per row\" the grid is 7–64 blocks → most SMs idle. But the total\n  work is minuscule (tens–hundreds of KB), so wall-time is dominated by **kernel-launch\n  latency and memory latency**, not throughput. The dominant win here is **fusion**:\n  the torch reference launches many kernels and materializes fp32 intermediates,\n  whereas a single fused Triton kernel is one launch reading bf16 once / writing once.\n- **Large (14418, 14509 rows).** ~113 MB read + ~113 MB written ⇒ ~226 MB of HBM\n  traffic (weight is 8 KB, cached, negligible). This regime is strictly\n  **memory-bandwidth-bound**; the objective is to hit the bf16-in/bf16-out roofline\n  and avoid the reference's extra fp32-intermediate traffic.\n\n**Implication:** a single, lean, fused, one-pass kernel should win in *both* regimes\nagainst the un-fused reference. The optimization headroom is (a) not leaving bandwidth\non the table for the large cases and (b) minimizing overhead for the tiny cases.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hardware roofline (A800, sm_80)\n\n- A800 = GA100 (108 SMs), sm_80, HBM2e. Peak HBM bandwidth ≈ **1.5–2.0 TB/s**\n  (≈2.0 TB/s for the 80 GB SKU, ≈1.55 TB/s for the 40 GB SKU); 40 MB L2.\n- Arithmetic intensity of RMSNorm is very low: ~2 FLOP-ish per element for the\n  square + a couple of multiplies, against 2 bytes read + 2 bytes written per element.\n  → **Memory bound.** FP32 ALU throughput is irrelevant at these sizes.\n\n**Ideal large-case time** (bf16 in + bf16 out, weight negligible):\n`226 MB / 1.8 TB/s ≈ 125 µs` (≈100 µs at 2.0 TB/s, ≈145 µs at 1.55 TB/s).\nThis is the target floor for batch≈14.5k. Any fused kernel that reads each input\nelement once and writes each output once, with coalesced accesses, should approach it.\n\n**Reference inefficiency:** the torch reference upcasts to fp32 (`x = …to(float32)`\nis a 4096·B·4-byte materialization = 2× the input traffic at fp32), then `x.pow(2)`,\nthen `.mean(...)`, then broadcast-mul twice, then downcast — multiple passes over\nlarge fp32 buffers plus several kernel launches. So the *speedup vs reference* on the\nlarge cases can be substantial purely from fusion + staying in bf16 for I/O.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Constraints (from CLAUDE.md / TASK.md)\n\n- **Triton is the primary implementation.** PyTorch only for metadata / launch\n  plumbing (shape, dtype, output allocation, grid). **No** Torch compute fallback,\n  **no** CPU/NumPy fallback, **no** CUDA-extension fallback, **no** alternate-impl\n  fallback. A failing Triton kernel is invalid and must be fixed as Triton.\n- **Immutable, sequential candidates** `c001, c002, …`; any meaningful source/config/\n  launch change ⇒ new candidate ID; never reuse an ID or rewrite past records.\n- **Evaluation only** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`; the five\n  fixed workloads = one candidate evaluation. Budget: 100 evals; token soft limit\n  1.0M / hard 1.2M. `final` is operator-approved only.\n- **I cannot run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, Python, or the evaluator directly.**\n  Local Bash is in \"don't ask\" / denied mode. ⇒ The *only* empirical signal is the\n  feedback evaluation. First-try correctness matters a lot; wasted evals cost tokens.\n- Skills `KernelWiki` (Blackwell/Hopper) and `ncu-report-skill` (B200/sm_100) are\n  **not applicable** to this sm_80 task and are not used.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Numerical risks & mitigations\n\n| Risk | Cause | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wrong accumulation precision | Squaring/summing in bf16 loses ~8 mantissa bits; 4096-term sum drifts | Load bf16 → `.to(tl.float32)` **before** squaring; reduce with fp32 `tl.sum`. |\n| Epsilon placement | Adding eps to RMS or to variance instead of to `E[x²]` | Compute `ms = sum(x²)/H`; `inv = rsqrt(ms + 1e-5)`. Match reference exactly. |\n| Wrong denominator | Using `sum` not `mean`, or wrong H | Divide by `H = 4096` (constexpr). |\n| Accidental centering | Copy-pasting a LayerNorm kernel | RMSNorm has **no** mean subtraction — do not subtract row mean. |\n| Multiply-order drift | `x*w*inv_rms` vs `(x*inv_rms)*w` giving ULP diffs | Follow reference order `(x*inv)*w`; keep all fp32 until final cast. Differences are sub-bf16-ULP and safe, but match to be conservative. |\n| Weight precision | Multiplying in bf16 | Upcast `weight` to fp32 before multiply (as reference does). |\n| Final cast / rounding | Writing fp32 or wrong rounding | Cast result to bf16 (Triton default round-to-nearest matches torch) and store to a bf16 output tensor. |\n| `rsqrt` accuracy | Fast-approx rsqrt could miss tolerance | Use fp32 `tl.rsqrt` (well within bf16 tolerance) or `1/tl.sqrt`; both are fp32-accurate enough. |\n| Non-contiguous input | Evaluator hands a strided tensor | Pass row stride explicitly (`x.stride(0)`), or `.contiguous()` as a metadata-only safeguard. Prefer stride args to avoid a copy. |\n| Overflow | `x²` overflow | bf16 range is huge; random inputs ⇒ no overflow. fp32 square is safe. |\n| Div-by-zero / all-zero row | `ms = 0` | `+1e-5` guarantees a finite `inv_rms`. |\n\n**Tolerance note:** `definition.json` does not expose explicit `rtol/atol`. Because I\ncannot see the checker thresholds and cannot test locally, the safe policy is to\n**bit-for-bit mirror the reference math** (fp32 accumulate, fp32 weight, same eps\nplacement, same op order, bf16 output). This maximizes the chance of passing whatever\nbf16 tolerance the official evaluator uses.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Triton design space\n\nAll designs share the fused body: load a row of bf16, upcast fp32, square, fp32\nreduce, `rsqrt(mean+eps)`, scale, multiply fp32 weight, cast bf16, store.\n\n### A. One-program-per-row, single tile (baseline; classic FlashInfer/vLLM/tutorial form)\n- `grid = (batch_size,)`; `BLOCK_SIZE = 4096` (= H, power of two → **no reduction mask**).\n- Each program: `offs = tl.arange(0, 4096)`; load row, `xf=x.to(fp32)`; `s=tl.sum(xf*xf)`;\n  `inv=tl.rsqrt(s/4096 + 1e-5)`; `y=(xf*inv)*w.to(fp32)`; store bf16.\n- Weight loaded once per program (resident in L2 across programs → cheap).\n- **Pros:** simplest, coalesced, one launch, bandwidth-optimal for large batch.\n  **Cons:** low SM occupancy for tiny batch (but that regime is latency-bound anyway).\n- **Tuning axes:** `num_warps ∈ {4, 8, 16}` (128/256/512 threads ⇒ 32/16/8 elems per\n  thread), `num_stages`. This is the primary workhorse; likely `c001`.\n\n### B. Autotuned variant of A\n- `triton.autotune` over `num_warps`/`num_stages` keyed on `batch_size` bucket, so the\n  tiny and large regimes each get their best config. Risk: autotune runs configs at\n  first call — must ensure the evaluator's timing excludes warmup, else noise. Treat as\n  a follow-up candidate once a good static config is known.\n\n### C. Multiple rows per program (row blocking)\n- Each program handles `R` rows (2D tile `[R, 4096]` or an inner loop), `grid = ceil(B/R)`.\n- **Pro:** fewer, larger blocks → can improve memory-level parallelism and amortize\n  per-program setup for the large cases; reduces grid size.\n- **Con:** for tiny batch it *reduces* parallelism (fewer blocks); register/SRAM\n  pressure grows with `R·4096`. Useful mainly as a large-batch tuning knob.\n\n### D. Split-reduction (split-K, two-pass) for the tiny cases\n- Split H into `K` chunks; pass 1 writes `K` partial sums per row (grid `(B, K)`),\n  pass 2 normalizes. For batch 7, `K=16` → 112 blocks → fills the machine.\n- **Con:** adds a second kernel launch and a global round-trip. Since the tiny regime\n  is *launch-latency* bound, a 2nd launch likely **hurts**, not helps. Keep as a\n  contingency only if the tiny-batch speedup underperforms and profiling-by-inference\n  says the reduction (not launch) is the bottleneck. **Low priority.**\n\n### E. Vectorization / access width\n- Rows are contiguous (stride 1), so `tl.arange(0,4096)` loads are naturally coalesced;\n  Triton vectorizes bf16 loads (up to 128-bit) automatically. Ensure the pointer math\n  uses the row stride and unit inner stride. Little to gain beyond A if already\n  coalesced, but verify no accidental masking/guard kills vectorization.\n\n### F. Grid-stride / persistent kernel\n- One block per SM looping over rows. Marginal benefit here; grid of 14.5k blocks is\n  already fine for A800 scheduling. Not worth the complexity unless launch-bound at\n  large batch (unlikely). **Deprioritized.**\n\n**Chosen direction:** start with **A** (static, well-chosen `num_warps`), confirm\ncorrectness + measure both regimes, then explore **B/C** as tuning refinements. Only\nconsider **D** if the tiny regime clearly lags.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Entry point / integration contract\n\n`solution/solution.py` must expose `run(hidden_states, weight)` returning the bf16\noutput tensor (mirroring the reference signature). Plumbing responsibilities (PyTorch\nallowed here, metadata only):\n\n- Read `B, H = hidden_states.shape`; assert `H == 4096`.\n- Allocate `out = torch.empty_like(hidden_states)` (bf16, same device).\n- Pass `stride(0)` for the row stride (both x and out), unit inner stride.\n- Compute `grid` from `B`; launch the Triton kernel; return `out`.\n- Do **not** synchronize unnecessarily; let the evaluator handle timing/sync.\n- Keep `EPS`, `H`, `BLOCK_SIZE` as constants/constexpr where possible.\n\nEdge cases to keep safe: `B` can be 7 (grid must be ≥1 and correct), and very large\n(14509 — well within 1D grid limits). No masking needed on the hidden axis because\n`BLOCK_SIZE == H == 4096`; batch axis uses `pid < B` implicitly via grid size.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Validation strategy (given no local execution)\n\nBecause the **only** empirical channel is `evaluate_candidate.sh feedback`, and every\neval costs tokens, I will front-load correctness by construction:\n\n1. **Static review before each eval:** re-check dtype casts (bf16→fp32 before square),\n   eps placement, denominator `H`, op order, bf16 store, stride/pointer math, grid,\n   constexpr usage, and that no Torch-compute fallback path exists.\n2. **Mirror reference math exactly** (Section 5) to de-risk the unseen tolerance.\n3. **c001 = correctness anchor:** simplest correct design A with a sane `num_warps`\n   (likely 8). Its purpose is to (a) confirm all five workloads PASS and (b) establish\n   baseline speedups per regime. Record everything in `candidates.jsonl`.\n4. **Optimize only after correctness is proven.** Change one variable per candidate\n   (`num_warps`, then `num_stages`, then row-blocking, then autotune) so each eval\n   yields an unambiguous signal. New ID per source change; never rewrite past records.\n5. **Read the evaluator output per workload** (pass/fail + speedup), attribute wins to\n   regimes (tiny vs large), and stop when the geomean converges or budget nears.\n6. **Convergence / stop:** when successive candidates no longer improve geomean\n   meaningfully (large case near the bandwidth roofline; tiny case near launch-latency\n   floor), write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the rationale. Never run `final` without\n   operator approval.\n\n**Interpreting results:** for the large cases, compare achieved time against the\n~100–145 µs roofline (Section 3) to know how much headroom remains. For the tiny\ncases, expect the fused single-launch kernel to already dominate the multi-launch\nreference; further kernel tuning yields little there, so don't overspend evals on it.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Risk register & open questions\n\n- **Unknown tolerance:** mitigated by exact-math mirroring; if a workload fails\n  correctness unexpectedly, first suspect eps placement, denominator, or a stray bf16\n  intermediate accumulation — not the tuning knobs.\n- **Autotune warmup vs. timing:** unclear whether the evaluator isolates warmup; prefer\n  a strong *static* config first, adopt autotune only if it demonstrably helps.\n- **Occupancy vs. work for tiny batch:** likely launch-bound; avoid over-engineering\n  split-K unless evidence says otherwise.\n- **Bandwidth SKU (40 vs 80 GB):** changes the absolute roofline but not the strategy;\n  the fused one-pass design is optimal regardless.\n- **Contiguity assumption:** inputs are freshly generated randoms (very likely\n  contiguous); still pass explicit strides so a strided input cannot silently break.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Direction summary (detail deferred to `docs/plan.md`)\n\n- **c001:** fused, one-pass, one-program-per-row Triton kernel, `BLOCK_SIZE=4096`,\n  fp32 accumulate, exact reference math, `num_warps=8` — correctness anchor + baseline.\n- **Then:** single-variable tuning — sweep `num_warps` (4/16) and `num_stages`;\n  consider row-blocking (design C) and/or autotune (design B) for the large regime.\n- **Contingency:** split-reduction (design D) only if the tiny regime underperforms.\n- **Stop** at geomean convergence (large near roofline, tiny near launch floor) and\n  write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`; `final` only with operator approval.\n</content>\n</invoke>\n","numLines":283,"startLine":1,"totalLines":283}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RGbCVZRXdD3Gq9Lv8c1DRD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Executable Optimization Plan — `rmsnorm_h4096` (FlashInfer, A800 / sm_80)\n2\t\n3\tDerived from `docs/draft.md`. This is the ordered, executable KDA plan. It defines the\n4\tcandidate lineage, the exact source/eval mechanics, per-candidate correctness checks,\n5\tfalsifiable performance hypotheses, the stopping criteria, and the evidence format for\n6\t`candidates.jsonl`. **No candidate is implemented or evaluated in this turn.**\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## 0. Ground rules (binding, from CLAUDE.md / TASK.md)\n11\t\n12\t- Primary implementation is **Triton**. PyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing\n13\t  (shape, dtype, output alloc, strides, grid). **No** Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension/\n14\t  alternate-impl compute fallback. A failing Triton kernel is invalid → fix as Triton,\n15\t  never fall back.\n16\t- Candidates are **immutable and sequential** (`c001`, `c002`, …). Any meaningful change\n17\t  to source, config, or launch ⇒ **new candidate ID**. Never reuse an ID; never rewrite a\n18\t  past `candidates.jsonl` record.\n19\t- Evaluate **only** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`. The five fixed\n20\t  feedback workloads together = **one** candidate evaluation.\n21\t- Budgets: **100** evaluations; token soft **1.0M** / normal **1.5M** / absolute **1.65M**.\n22\t- I **cannot** run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, Python, the evaluator directly, or any\n23\t  alternate correctness harness. The **only** empirical signal is the feedback eval.\n24\t  ⇒ Front-load correctness by construction; spend evals deliberately.\n25\t- `final` (14-workload) runs **only** with explicit operator approval. Never auto-run it.\n26\t- `KernelWiki` skill is Blackwell/Hopper-specific ⇒ **not applicable** to sm_80; not used.\n27\t  (Recorded as `skill_usage: none` per candidate.)\n28\t\n29\t---\n30\t\n31\t## 1. Operation contract (what every candidate must compute)\n32\t\n33\tPer row `i` of `x[B, H]`, `H = 4096` (compile-time constant), `EPS = 1e-5`:\n34\t\n35\t```\n36\tms_i      = (1/H) * Σ_j (fp32(x_ij))^2      # upcast bf16→fp32 BEFORE squaring; fp32 reduce\n37\tinv_rms_i = rsqrt(ms_i + 1e-5)              # fp32\n38\ty_ij      = (fp32(x_ij) * inv_rms_i) * fp32(w_j)   # fp32 throughout; order (x*inv)*w\n39\tout_ij    = bf16(y_ij)                      # single final downcast; RNE\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\tNon-negotiable numerics (mirror the reference to de-risk the unseen tolerance):\n43\tfp32 accumulation, denominator `H=4096` (true mean), `+1e-5` added to the **mean of\n44\tsquares**, **no** mean-subtraction (RMSNorm, not LayerNorm), weight upcast to fp32,\n45\tmultiply order `(x*inv)*w`, single bf16 store.\n46\t\n47\tEntry point: `solution/solution.py` exposing `run(hidden_states, weight) -> out` (bf16,\n48\tsame shape/device as input).\n49\t\n50\t---\n51\t\n52\t## 2. Source & evaluation mechanics (per candidate)\n53\t\n54\tFor each candidate `cNNN` the implement/eval turn will:\n55\t\n56\t1. Write the candidate's Triton kernel + `run(...)` plumbing to `solution/solution.py`.\n57\t2. Snapshot it immutably to `runs/candidates/cNNN/solution.py` (copy, never edit in place\n58\t   afterward) and record its source hash — this backs the \"immutable ID + hash\" contract\n59\t   and the `source_hash` evidence field. If the launcher expects the source only at\n60\t   `solution/solution.py`, keep both in sync at creation time and never mutate the\n61\t   snapshot afterward.\n62\t3. Run `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` **once**.\n63\t4. Parse per-workload PASS/FAIL + speedup, compute geomean, append **one** JSON record to\n64\t   `candidates.jsonl` (Section 7). Never modify prior records.\n65\t\n66\tRule: if a change is only in `num_warps`/`num_stages`/tile/launch, it is still a **new\n67\tcandidate ID** because launch/config changed. One source version per ID, always.\n68\t\n69\t---\n70\t\n71\t## 3. Candidate lineage strategy\n72\t\n73\tLinear backbone with single-variable steps so each eval yields an unambiguous signal.\n74\tEach node names its **parent**, the **one** variable changed, and its **hypothesis**.\n75\tLater branches are conditional on observed evidence; exact IDs assigned as we go.\n76\t\n77\t```\n78\tc001 (anchor: design A, num_warps=8)\n79\t  └─ c002  num_warps sweep  (try the more promising of {4,16} first)\n80\t       └─ c003  num_warps sweep  (the other of {4,16}) — fix best warps W*\n81\t            └─ c004  num_stages tuning at W*  (e.g. 2→3/4 if it helps large regime)\n82\t                 └─ c005  row-blocking (design C) for the large regime  [conditional]\n83\t                      └─ c006  autotune (design B) keyed on batch bucket [conditional]\n84\t                           └─ c007  split-reduction (design D) for tiny [contingency only]\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\t- **c001 — correctness anchor & baseline (design A).** Fused, one-pass,\n88\t  one-program-per-row; `grid=(B,)`; `BLOCK_SIZE = H = 4096` (power of two ⇒ **no\n89\t  reduction mask** on the hidden axis); fp32 accumulate; exact reference math; explicit\n90\t  row strides; `num_warps=8`, `num_stages=default`. Purpose: prove all five workloads\n91\t  PASS and establish per-regime baseline speedups. **Gate: do not tune until c001 passes.**\n92\t- **c002 / c003 — `num_warps` sweep.** Change only `num_warps` (128/256/512 threads ⇒\n93\t  32/16/8 fp32 elems/thread). This is the single biggest bandwidth knob for the large\n94\t  regime. Pick the winner `W*`.\n95\t- **c004 — `num_stages`.** At `W*`, try increasing pipeline stages to overlap global\n96\t  loads with compute on the large cases. Keep whichever wins.\n97\t- **c005 — row-blocking (design C).** Each program handles `R` rows (inner loop / 2D\n98\t  tile), `grid=ceil(B/R)`. Only pursued if the large regime remains below roofline and\n99\t  evidence suggests grid/setup overhead or MLP underutilization. Watch register/SRAM\n100\t  pressure (`R·4096` fp32).\n101\t- **c006 — autotune (design B).** `triton.autotune` over `num_warps`/`num_stages`/`R`\n102\t  keyed on a `batch_size` bucket so tiny and large regimes each get their best config.\n103\t  Only if a single static config cannot serve both regimes well **and** we confirm the\n104\t  evaluator's timing excludes autotune warmup (else it adds noise; if unclear, prefer the\n105\t  best static config and skip autotune).\n106\t- **c007 — split-reduction (design D).** Contingency **only** if the tiny regime clearly\n107\t  lags and evidence indicates the reduction (not launch latency) is the bottleneck. Adds a\n108\t  second launch + global round-trip, which likely hurts the launch-bound tiny regime, so\n109\t  this is low priority and may never be built.\n110\t\n111\tDeprioritized (from draft, not scheduled unless evidence forces it): grid-stride/\n112\tpersistent kernel (design F), manual vectorization beyond Triton's automatic bf16\n113\tcoalescing (design E) — the contiguous unit-stride rows already vectorize.\n114\t\n115\t---\n116\t\n117\t## 4. Per-candidate correctness checklist (static, before every eval)\n118\t\n119\tBecause the only empirical channel costs tokens, run this static review on the source\n120\tbefore each evaluation:\n121\t\n122\t1. **Cast order:** bf16 load → `.to(tl.float32)` **before** squaring; reduce with fp32\n123\t   `tl.sum`. No bf16 accumulation anywhere.\n124\t2. **Epsilon:** `inv = tl.rsqrt(sum_sq / 4096.0 + 1e-5)` — eps on the mean-of-squares,\n125\t   not on variance and not on RMS. `1e-5` hardcoded as fp32.\n126\t3. **Denominator:** divide by `H = 4096` (constexpr), a true mean.\n127\t4. **No centering:** confirm no row-mean subtraction slipped in from a LayerNorm template.\n128\t5. **Multiply order & dtypes:** `y = (xf * inv) * w_f`, `w` upcast to fp32; all fp32 until\n129\t   the final cast.\n130\t6. **Final store:** cast to bf16 (RNE) into a bf16 output tensor; no fp32 output.\n131\t7. **Pointer/stride math:** use `x.stride(0)` for the row stride, unit inner stride for\n132\t   both input and output; `BLOCK_SIZE == H == 4096` so no hidden-axis mask.\n133\t8. **Grid & bounds:** `grid=(B,)` correct for `B ∈ {7, 14, 15, 64, 14418, 14509}`; program\n134\t   id maps 1:1 to a valid row (no OOB); handles the smallest `B` and 1D-grid-legal for the\n135\t   largest.\n136\t9. **Constants:** `EPS`, `H`, `BLOCK_SIZE` are constants/`constexpr`.\n137\t10. **No fallback path:** `run(...)` has exactly one compute path (the Triton launch); no\n138\t    Torch/NumPy/CUDA-ext branch, no `try/except` that computes an alternate result.\n139\t11. **Metadata-only Torch:** only shape/dtype/alloc/stride/grid use Torch; optionally a\n140\t    `.contiguous()` **safeguard** only if a strided input is possible (prefer explicit\n141\t    strides to avoid a copy). No compute in Torch.\n142\t12. **Determinism:** no data-dependent control flow that changes numerics across rows.\n143\t\n144\tCorrectness is defined by the **official evaluator's** per-workload PASS/FAIL (its\n145\ttolerance is unseen; mirroring reference math in fp32 is the mitigation). A candidate with\n146\tany FAIL is **not** eligible as the best/selected candidate regardless of speedup.\n147\t\n148\t---\n149\t\n150\t## 5. Performance hypotheses (falsifiable, tied to the roofline)\n151\t\n152\tRoofline (draft §3): large cases move ~226 MB HBM (≈113 MB read + ≈113 MB write; weight\n153\t8 KB negligible) ⇒ ideal ≈ **100–145 µs** (≈125 µs @1.8 TB/s). Tiny cases are\n154\t**launch/latency-bound** (tens–hundreds of KB total).\n155\t\n156\t- **H1 (fusion win, both regimes).** A single fused one-pass bf16-in/bf16-out kernel beats\n157\t  the un-fused torch reference (which materializes fp32 intermediates and launches several\n158\t  kernels) in **all five** workloads. *Falsified if* any workload shows speedup ≤ 1.0×.\n159\t- **H2 (large regime near roofline).** c001's large-case time is within a modest factor of\n160\t  the 100–145 µs floor; achievable speedup vs reference is large because the reference\n161\t  pays extra fp32-intermediate traffic. *Measure* achieved-vs-roofline to size headroom.\n162\t- **H3 (`num_warps` matters for large, not tiny).** Sweeping `num_warps` moves the large\n163\t  regime (bandwidth/occupancy) but barely moves tiny (launch-bound). *Falsified if* tiny\n164\t  swings more than large under warp changes.\n165\t- **H4 (`num_stages` overlap).** More pipeline stages help the large regime by overlapping\n166\t  loads with compute, up to SRAM/occupancy limits. *Falsified if* extra stages don't\n167\t  improve or regress large-case time.\n168\t- **H5 (row-blocking, large only).** Row-blocking helps the large regime (fewer/larger\n169\t  blocks, better MLP) but **hurts** tiny (fewer blocks ⇒ less parallelism). *Falsified if*\n170\t  it fails to improve large or it improves tiny.\n171\t- **H6 (split-K hurts tiny).** A two-pass split-reduction adds a launch + round-trip and\n172\t  therefore does **not** help the launch-bound tiny regime. *Falsified if* c007 improves\n173\t  tiny geomean over the best single-pass kernel. (Also gates whether c007 is ever built.)\n174\t\n175\tInterpretation policy: attribute each eval's deltas to the tiny vs large regime; compare\n176\tlarge-case times to the roofline to decide remaining headroom; don't overspend evals on\n177\tthe tiny regime once it's launch-floor-bound.\n178\t\n179\t---\n180\t\n181\t## 6. Stopping criteria (convergence)\n182\t\n183\tStop and stop-tuning when **any** of:\n184\t\n185\t1. **Roofline + floor reached:** large-case time is close to the 100–145 µs HBM floor\n186\t   *and* tiny-case time is at the launch-latency floor (single fused launch), i.e. no\n187\t   plausible mechanism remains.\n188\t2. **Geomean convergence:** two or three successive candidates fail to improve the feedback\n189\t   geomean by a meaningful margin (≲ ~2%), i.e. diminishing returns.\n190\t3. **Budget guard:** approaching the evaluation budget (≤ a few of the 100 evals left) or\n191\t   the token soft limit (1.0M) — then lock in the best valid candidate and stop.\n192\t4. **All hypotheses resolved:** the scheduled/conditional candidates are exhausted and none\n193\t   beats the current best valid candidate.\n194\t\n195\tOn stop: identify the **best valid candidate** (highest feedback geomean with **all five**\n196\tworkloads PASS), write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the rationale (which criterion, best ID, its\n197\tgeomean, per-regime standing vs roofline/launch floor). Do **not** run `final` — it is\n198\toperator-approved only; note that the best candidate is ready for operator-approved final.\n199\t\n200\t---\n201\t\n202\t## 7. Evidence format (one JSON object per candidate, appended to `candidates.jsonl`)\n203\t\n204\tAppend exactly one line per evaluated candidate; never rewrite earlier lines. Schema:\n205\t\n206\t```json\n207\t{\n208\t  \"candidate_id\": \"c001\",\n209\t  \"parent_id\": null,\n210\t  \"source_path\": \"runs/candidates/c001/solution.py\",\n211\t  \"source_hash\": \"<sha256 of the immutable candidate source>\",\n212\t  \"design\": \"A: fused one-pass, one-program-per-row, BLOCK_SIZE=4096\",\n213\t  \"config\": {\"num_warps\": 8, \"num_stages\": null, \"rows_per_program\": 1, \"BLOCK_SIZE\": 4096},\n214\t  \"changed_vs_parent\": \"initial anchor\",\n215\t  \"hypothesis\": \"H1/H2: fused one-pass kernel passes all 5 and beats reference in both regimes\",\n216\t  \"static_checks\": {\"casts_fp32\": true, \"eps_on_meansq\": true, \"denom_H\": true,\n217\t                    \"no_centering\": true, \"mul_order_x_inv_w\": true, \"bf16_store\": true,\n218\t                    \"strides_ok\": true, \"grid_ok\": true, \"no_fallback\": true},\n219\t  \"per_workload\": [\n220\t    {\"uuid\": \"9d403d2b-7859-4dab-aaf4-12e53c555001\", \"batch_size\": 15,    \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null},\n221\t    {\"uuid\": \"14531e96-f6e6-4515-abb4-10855f72c80e\", \"batch_size\": 64,    \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null},\n222\t    {\"uuid\": \"841b0afa-80fa-449a-9e1d-f294da92d02f\", \"batch_size\": 14509, \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null},\n223\t    {\"uuid\": \"33bf737d-3b37-4e38-be80-ea39b4b46ae6\", \"batch_size\": 7,     \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null},\n224\t    {\"uuid\": \"f0f508c3-e880-4ec8-b8be-1062db313d36\", \"batch_size\": 14418, \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null}\n225\t  ],\n226\t  \"all_pass\": true,\n227\t  \"geomean_speedup\": null,\n228\t  \"regime_notes\": {\"tiny\": \"…\", \"large\": \"… vs ~100-145us roofline\"},\n229\t  \"decision\": \"keep|reject|new-best|baseline\",\n230\t  \"next_action\": \"c002: num_warps sweep\",\n231\t  \"cumulative_evals\": 1,\n232\t  \"skill_usage\": \"none\",\n233\t  \"tokens_note\": \"optional running token observation\"\n234\t}\n235\t```\n236\t\n237\tField rules:\n238\t- `speedup`/`time_us` filled from the evaluator's actual output (units as reported); leave\n239\t  `null` only if the evaluator does not report that field.\n240\t- `geomean_speedup` = geometric mean of the five per-workload speedups (only meaningful if\n241\t  `all_pass` is true; if any FAIL, mark `decision: \"reject\"` and geomean as reported/`null`).\n242\t- `parent_id` names the lineage parent (Section 3); `changed_vs_parent` states the single\n243\t  variable changed.\n244\t- `cumulative_evals` is the running count of feedback evaluations used (max 100).\n245\t- `decision`: `new-best` iff `all_pass` and geomean strictly beats the prior best valid\n246\t  candidate; else `keep`/`reject`/`baseline` as appropriate.\n247\t\n248\t---\n249\t\n250\t## 8. Turn-by-turn execution loop (what each future turn does)\n251\t\n252\t1. Pick the next candidate ID per Section 3 (respecting conditionals/contingencies).\n253\t2. Implement its single-variable change in `solution/solution.py`; snapshot to\n254\t   `runs/candidates/cNNN/solution.py`; record source hash.\n255\t3. Run the Section 4 static checklist; fix statically before spending an eval.\n256\t4. `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (once).\n257\t5. Parse results; append one `candidates.jsonl` record (Section 7); update the best-valid\n258\t   tracker; note tokens/evals used.\n259\t6. Decide next action via hypotheses (Section 5) and stopping criteria (Section 6).\n260\t7. On convergence/budget: write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`; do **not** run `final`.\n261\t\n262\t---\n263\t\n264\t## 9. First concrete step (next turn)\n265\t\n266\tImplement **c001** (design A) exactly per Sections 1–4: fused one-pass, one-program-per-row,\n267\t`BLOCK_SIZE=4096`, fp32 accumulate, exact reference numerics, explicit strides, no mask on\n268\tthe hidden axis, `num_warps=8`, single bf16 store, no fallback path. Run the static\n269\tchecklist, evaluate once on feedback, and record the anchor/baseline. Tune only after c001\n270\tpasses all five workloads.\n271\t\n272\t---\n273\t\n274\t## 10. Decision log\n275\t\n276\t### c001 — DONE (eval #1). Baseline. All 5 PASS, geomean **4.55x**.\n277\tEvaluator ground truth (previously unseen): device **A800-SXM4-80GB**, tolerance\n278\t`atol=0.01 rtol=0.01 matched_ratio=0.99`, `warmup=3 iters=100` (so autotune warmup should\n279\tbe excluded → design B remains viable). Per-workload:\n280\t- tiny b7/15/64: sol **~39–40 µs**, **2.72–2.80x** vs ref. Bit-exact (abs=rel=0).\n281\t- large b14418/14509: sol **~163–164 µs**, **9.57–9.62x** vs ref, abs=3.12e-2 rel~7.8e-3\n282\t  (well inside tol). Roofline: 226 MB / 2.0 TB/s ≈ **113 µs** ⇒ c001 large ≈ **69%** of\n283\t  peak BW ⇒ real headroom for the large regime (H2 confirmed with room).\n284\t\n285\tHypothesis status: **H1 confirmed** (fused kernel beats reference in all 5). **H2 confirmed\n286\twith headroom** (large ≈69% of roofline).\n287\t\n288\tRead on regimes:\n289\t- Tiny cases are floored at ~39–40 µs regardless of `batch_size` (7→64 nearly identical),\n290\t  i.e. **launch/fixed-overhead bound**, essentially independent of the kernel body. Little\n291\t  to gain here; avoid overspending evals (do NOT build split-K / design D unless something\n292\t  changes — H6 stands).\n293\t- Large cases carry the optimization weight and dominate geomean upside. Priority = push\n294\t  the large regime toward the 113 µs floor.\n295\t\n296\t### Next: c002 — `num_warps` sweep, try **16** first (parent c001).\n297\tRationale: at `num_warps=8`, each program (BLOCK_SIZE=4096 fp32) uses 8 elems/thread;\n298\tmore warps (16 → 4 elems/thread) can raise per-SM memory-level parallelism and better\n299\tsaturate HBM on the large regime (H3). Change **only** `num_warps=8→16`; new ID c002.\n300\tIf 16 wins, c003 confirms trend / tries 32 or backs off; if 16 loses, c003 tries 4. Then\n301\tc004 tunes `num_stages` at the best warps. Row-blocking (c005) is the main lever if warp/\n302\tstage tuning stalls below roofline. Stop per Section 6 when large nears ~113 µs or geomean\n303\tconverges.\n304\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d241f2d5-b247-435b-b5e7-bf68463d035a","uuid":"f23c4121-d4cc-4dc3-8683-d1a47c5b1598","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:20:37.932Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--flashinfer--rmsnorm_h4096/docs/plan.md","content":"# Executable Optimization Plan — `rmsnorm_h4096` (FlashInfer, A800 / sm_80)\n\nDerived from `docs/draft.md`. This is the ordered, executable KDA plan. It defines the\ncandidate lineage, the exact source/eval mechanics, per-candidate correctness checks,\nfalsifiable performance hypotheses, the stopping criteria, and the evidence format for\n`candidates.jsonl`. **No candidate is implemented or evaluated in this turn.**\n\n---\n\n## 0. Ground rules (binding, from CLAUDE.md / TASK.md)\n\n- Primary implementation is **Triton**. PyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing\n  (shape, dtype, output alloc, strides, grid). **No** Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension/\n  alternate-impl compute fallback. A failing Triton kernel is invalid → fix as Triton,\n  never fall back.\n- Candidates are **immutable and sequential** (`c001`, `c002`, …). Any meaningful change\n  to source, config, or launch ⇒ **new candidate ID**. Never reuse an ID; never rewrite a\n  past `candidates.jsonl` record.\n- Evaluate **only** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`. The five fixed\n  feedback workloads together = **one** candidate evaluation.\n- Budgets: **100** evaluations; token soft **1.0M** / normal **1.5M** / absolute **1.65M**.\n- I **cannot** run CUDA, a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, Python, the evaluator directly, or any\n  alternate correctness harness. The **only** empirical signal is the feedback eval.\n  ⇒ Front-load correctness by construction; spend evals deliberately.\n- `final` (14-workload) runs **only** with explicit operator approval. Never auto-run it.\n- `KernelWiki` skill is Blackwell/Hopper-specific ⇒ **not applicable** to sm_80; not used.\n  (Recorded as `skill_usage: none` per candidate.)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation contract (what every candidate must compute)\n\nPer row `i` of `x[B, H]`, `H = 4096` (compile-time constant), `EPS = 1e-5`:\n\n```\nms_i      = (1/H) * Σ_j (fp32(x_ij))^2      # upcast bf16→fp32 BEFORE squaring; fp32 reduce\ninv_rms_i = rsqrt(ms_i + 1e-5)              # fp32\ny_ij      = (fp32(x_ij) * inv_rms_i) * fp32(w_j)   # fp32 throughout; order (x*inv)*w\nout_ij    = bf16(y_ij)                      # single final downcast; RNE\n```\n\nNon-negotiable numerics (mirror the reference to de-risk the unseen tolerance):\nfp32 accumulation, denominator `H=4096` (true mean), `+1e-5` added to the **mean of\nsquares**, **no** mean-subtraction (RMSNorm, not LayerNorm), weight upcast to fp32,\nmultiply order `(x*inv)*w`, single bf16 store.\n\nEntry point: `solution/solution.py` exposing `run(hidden_states, weight) -> out` (bf16,\nsame shape/device as input).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Source & evaluation mechanics (per candidate)\n\nFor each candidate `cNNN` the implement/eval turn will:\n\n1. Write the candidate's Triton kernel + `run(...)` plumbing to `solution/solution.py`.\n2. Snapshot it immutably to `runs/candidates/cNNN/solution.py` (copy, never edit in place\n   afterward) and record its source hash — this backs the \"immutable ID + hash\" contract\n   and the `source_hash` evidence field. If the launcher expects the source only at\n   `solution/solution.py`, keep both in sync at creation time and never mutate the\n   snapshot afterward.\n3. Run `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` **once**.\n4. Parse per-workload PASS/FAIL + speedup, compute geomean, append **one** JSON record to\n   `candidates.jsonl` (Section 7). Never modify prior records.\n\nRule: if a change is only in `num_warps`/`num_stages`/tile/launch, it is still a **new\ncandidate ID** because launch/config changed. One source version per ID, always.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Candidate lineage strategy\n\nLinear backbone with single-variable steps so each eval yields an unambiguous signal.\nEach node names its **parent**, the **one** variable changed, and its **hypothesis**.\nLater branches are conditional on observed evidence; exact IDs assigned as we go.\n\n```\nc001 (anchor: design A, num_warps=8)\n  └─ c002  num_warps sweep  (try the more promising of {4,16} first)\n       └─ c003  num_warps sweep  (the other of {4,16}) — fix best warps W*\n            └─ c004  num_stages tuning at W*  (e.g. 2→3/4 if it helps large regime)\n                 └─ c005  row-blocking (design C) for the large regime  [conditional]\n                      └─ c006  autotune (design B) keyed on batch bucket [conditional]\n                           └─ c007  split-reduction (design D) for tiny [contingency only]\n```\n\n- **c001 — correctness anchor & baseline (design A).** Fused, one-pass,\n  one-program-per-row; `grid=(B,)`; `BLOCK_SIZE = H = 4096` (power of two ⇒ **no\n  reduction mask** on the hidden axis); fp32 accumulate; exact reference math; explicit\n  row strides; `num_warps=8`, `num_stages=default`. Purpose: prove all five workloads\n  PASS and establish per-regime baseline speedups. **Gate: do not tune until c001 passes.**\n- **c002 / c003 — `num_warps` sweep.** Change only `num_warps` (128/256/512 threads ⇒\n  32/16/8 fp32 elems/thread). This is the single biggest bandwidth knob for the large\n  regime. Pick the winner `W*`.\n- **c004 — `num_stages`.** At `W*`, try increasing pipeline stages to overlap global\n  loads with compute on the large cases. Keep whichever wins.\n- **c005 — row-blocking (design C).** Each program handles `R` rows (inner loop / 2D\n  tile), `grid=ceil(B/R)`. Only pursued if the large regime remains below roofline and\n  evidence suggests grid/setup overhead or MLP underutilization. Watch register/SRAM\n  pressure (`R·4096` fp32).\n- **c006 — autotune (design B).** `triton.autotune` over `num_warps`/`num_stages`/`R`\n  keyed on a `batch_size` bucket so tiny and large regimes each get their best config.\n  Only if a single static config cannot serve both regimes well **and** we confirm the\n  evaluator's timing excludes autotune warmup (else it adds noise; if unclear, prefer the\n  best static config and skip autotune).\n- **c007 — split-reduction (design D).** Contingency **only** if the tiny regime clearly\n  lags and evidence indicates the reduction (not launch latency) is the bottleneck. Adds a\n  second launch + global round-trip, which likely hurts the launch-bound tiny regime, so\n  this is low priority and may never be built.\n\nDeprioritized (from draft, not scheduled unless evidence forces it): grid-stride/\npersistent kernel (design F), manual vectorization beyond Triton's automatic bf16\ncoalescing (design E) — the contiguous unit-stride rows already vectorize.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Per-candidate correctness checklist (static, before every eval)\n\nBecause the only empirical channel costs tokens, run this static review on the source\nbefore each evaluation:\n\n1. **Cast order:** bf16 load → `.to(tl.float32)` **before** squaring; reduce with fp32\n   `tl.sum`. No bf16 accumulation anywhere.\n2. **Epsilon:** `inv = tl.rsqrt(sum_sq / 4096.0 + 1e-5)` — eps on the mean-of-squares,\n   not on variance and not on RMS. `1e-5` hardcoded as fp32.\n3. **Denominator:** divide by `H = 4096` (constexpr), a true mean.\n4. **No centering:** confirm no row-mean subtraction slipped in from a LayerNorm template.\n5. **Multiply order & dtypes:** `y = (xf * inv) * w_f`, `w` upcast to fp32; all fp32 until\n   the final cast.\n6. **Final store:** cast to bf16 (RNE) into a bf16 output tensor; no fp32 output.\n7. **Pointer/stride math:** use `x.stride(0)` for the row stride, unit inner stride for\n   both input and output; `BLOCK_SIZE == H == 4096` so no hidden-axis mask.\n8. **Grid & bounds:** `grid=(B,)` correct for `B ∈ {7, 14, 15, 64, 14418, 14509}`; program\n   id maps 1:1 to a valid row (no OOB); handles the smallest `B` and 1D-grid-legal for the\n   largest.\n9. **Constants:** `EPS`, `H`, `BLOCK_SIZE` are constants/`constexpr`.\n10. **No fallback path:** `run(...)` has exactly one compute path (the Triton launch); no\n    Torch/NumPy/CUDA-ext branch, no `try/except` that computes an alternate result.\n11. **Metadata-only Torch:** only shape/dtype/alloc/stride/grid use Torch; optionally a\n    `.contiguous()` **safeguard** only if a strided input is possible (prefer explicit\n    strides to avoid a copy). No compute in Torch.\n12. **Determinism:** no data-dependent control flow that changes numerics across rows.\n\nCorrectness is defined by the **official evaluator's** per-workload PASS/FAIL (its\ntolerance is unseen; mirroring reference math in fp32 is the mitigation). A candidate with\nany FAIL is **not** eligible as the best/selected candidate regardless of speedup.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Performance hypotheses (falsifiable, tied to the roofline)\n\nRoofline (draft §3): large cases move ~226 MB HBM (≈113 MB read + ≈113 MB write; weight\n8 KB negligible) ⇒ ideal ≈ **100–145 µs** (≈125 µs @1.8 TB/s). Tiny cases are\n**launch/latency-bound** (tens–hundreds of KB total).\n\n- **H1 (fusion win, both regimes).** A single fused one-pass bf16-in/bf16-out kernel beats\n  the un-fused torch reference (which materializes fp32 intermediates and launches several\n  kernels) in **all five** workloads. *Falsified if* any workload shows speedup ≤ 1.0×.\n- **H2 (large regime near roofline).** c001's large-case time is within a modest factor of\n  the 100–145 µs floor; achievable speedup vs reference is large because the reference\n  pays extra fp32-intermediate traffic. *Measure* achieved-vs-roofline to size headroom.\n- **H3 (`num_warps` matters for large, not tiny).** Sweeping `num_warps` moves the large\n  regime (bandwidth/occupancy) but barely moves tiny (launch-bound). *Falsified if* tiny\n  swings more than large under warp changes.\n- **H4 (`num_stages` overlap).** More pipeline stages help the large regime by overlapping\n  loads with compute, up to SRAM/occupancy limits. *Falsified if* extra stages don't\n  improve or regress large-case time.\n- **H5 (row-blocking, large only).** Row-blocking helps the large regime (fewer/larger\n  blocks, better MLP) but **hurts** tiny (fewer blocks ⇒ less parallelism). *Falsified if*\n  it fails to improve large or it improves tiny.\n- **H6 (split-K hurts tiny).** A two-pass split-reduction adds a launch + round-trip and\n  therefore does **not** help the launch-bound tiny regime. *Falsified if* c007 improves\n  tiny geomean over the best single-pass kernel. (Also gates whether c007 is ever built.)\n\nInterpretation policy: attribute each eval's deltas to the tiny vs large regime; compare\nlarge-case times to the roofline to decide remaining headroom; don't overspend evals on\nthe tiny regime once it's launch-floor-bound.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Stopping criteria (convergence)\n\nStop and stop-tuning when **any** of:\n\n1. **Roofline + floor reached:** large-case time is close to the 100–145 µs HBM floor\n   *and* tiny-case time is at the launch-latency floor (single fused launch), i.e. no\n   plausible mechanism remains.\n2. **Geomean convergence:** two or three successive candidates fail to improve the feedback\n   geomean by a meaningful margin (≲ ~2%), i.e. diminishing returns.\n3. **Budget guard:** approaching the evaluation budget (≤ a few of the 100 evals left) or\n   the token soft limit (1.0M) — then lock in the best valid candidate and stop.\n4. **All hypotheses resolved:** the scheduled/conditional candidates are exhausted and none\n   beats the current best valid candidate.\n\nOn stop: identify the **best valid candidate** (highest feedback geomean with **all five**\nworkloads PASS), write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the rationale (which criterion, best ID, its\ngeomean, per-regime standing vs roofline/launch floor). Do **not** run `final` — it is\noperator-approved only; note that the best candidate is ready for operator-approved final.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Evidence format (one JSON object per candidate, appended to `candidates.jsonl`)\n\nAppend exactly one line per evaluated candidate; never rewrite earlier lines. Schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"candidate_id\": \"c001\",\n  \"parent_id\": null,\n  \"source_path\": \"runs/candidates/c001/solution.py\",\n  \"source_hash\": \"<sha256 of the immutable candidate source>\",\n  \"design\": \"A: fused one-pass, one-program-per-row, BLOCK_SIZE=4096\",\n  \"config\": {\"num_warps\": 8, \"num_stages\": null, \"rows_per_program\": 1, \"BLOCK_SIZE\": 4096},\n  \"changed_vs_parent\": \"initial anchor\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"H1/H2: fused one-pass kernel passes all 5 and beats reference in both regimes\",\n  \"static_checks\": {\"casts_fp32\": true, \"eps_on_meansq\": true, \"denom_H\": true,\n                    \"no_centering\": true, \"mul_order_x_inv_w\": true, \"bf16_store\": true,\n                    \"strides_ok\": true, \"grid_ok\": true, \"no_fallback\": true},\n  \"per_workload\": [\n    {\"uuid\": \"9d403d2b-7859-4dab-aaf4-12e53c555001\", \"batch_size\": 15,    \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null},\n    {\"uuid\": \"14531e96-f6e6-4515-abb4-10855f72c80e\", \"batch_size\": 64,    \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null},\n    {\"uuid\": \"841b0afa-80fa-449a-9e1d-f294da92d02f\", \"batch_size\": 14509, \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null},\n    {\"uuid\": \"33bf737d-3b37-4e38-be80-ea39b4b46ae6\", \"batch_size\": 7,     \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null},\n    {\"uuid\": \"f0f508c3-e880-4ec8-b8be-1062db313d36\", \"batch_size\": 14418, \"pass\": true, \"speedup\": null, \"time_us\": null}\n  ],\n  \"all_pass\": true,\n  \"geomean_speedup\": null,\n  \"regime_notes\": {\"tiny\": \"…\", \"large\": \"… vs ~100-145us roofline\"},\n  \"decision\": \"keep|reject|new-best|baseline\",\n  \"next_action\": \"c002: num_warps sweep\",\n  \"cumulative_evals\": 1,\n  \"skill_usage\": \"none\",\n  \"tokens_note\": \"optional running token observation\"\n}\n```\n\nField rules:\n- `speedup`/`time_us` filled from the evaluator's actual output (units as reported); leave\n  `null` only if the evaluator does not report that field.\n- `geomean_speedup` = geometric mean of the five per-workload speedups (only meaningful if\n  `all_pass` is true; if any FAIL, mark `decision: \"reject\"` and geomean as reported/`null`).\n- `parent_id` names the lineage parent (Section 3); `changed_vs_parent` states the single\n  variable changed.\n- `cumulative_evals` is the running count of feedback evaluations used (max 100).\n- `decision`: `new-best` iff `all_pass` and geomean strictly beats the prior best valid\n  candidate; else `keep`/`reject`/`baseline` as appropriate.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Turn-by-turn execution loop (what each future turn does)\n\n1. Pick the next candidate ID per Section 3 (respecting conditionals/contingencies).\n2. Implement its single-variable change in `solution/solution.py`; snapshot to\n   `runs/candidates/cNNN/solution.py`; record source hash.\n3. Run the Section 4 static checklist; fix statically before spending an eval.\n4. `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (once).\n5. Parse results; append one `candidates.jsonl` record (Section 7); update the best-valid\n   tracker; note tokens/evals used.\n6. Decide next action via hypotheses (Section 5) and stopping criteria (Section 6).\n7. On convergence/budget: write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`; do **not** run `final`.\n\n---\n\n## 9. First concrete step (next turn)\n\nImplement **c001** (design A) exactly per Sections 1–4: fused one-pass, one-program-per-row,\n`BLOCK_SIZE=4096`, fp32 accumulate, exact reference numerics, explicit strides, no mask on\nthe hidden axis, `num_warps=8`, single bf16 store, no fallback path. Run the static\nchecklist, evaluate once on feedback, and record the anchor/baseline. Tune only after c001\npasses all five workloads.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Decision log\n\n### c001 — DONE (eval #1). Baseline. All 5 PASS, geomean **4.55x**.\nEvaluator ground truth (previously unseen): device **A800-SXM4-80GB**, tolerance\n`atol=0.01 rtol=0.01 matched_ratio=0.99`, `warmup=3 iters=100` (so autotune warmup should\nbe excluded → design B remains viable). Per-workload:\n- tiny b7/15/64: sol **~39–40 µs**, **2.72–2.80x** vs ref. Bit-exact (abs=rel=0).\n- large b14418/14509: sol **~163–164 µs**, **9.57–9.62x** vs ref, abs=3.12e-2 rel~7.8e-3\n  (well inside tol). Roofline: 226 MB / 2.0 TB/s ≈ **113 µs** ⇒ c001 large ≈ **69%** of\n  peak BW ⇒ real headroom for the large regime (H2 confirmed with room).\n\nHypothesis status: **H1 confirmed** (fused kernel beats reference in all 5). **H2 confirmed\nwith headroom** (large ≈69% of roofline).\n\nRead on regimes:\n- Tiny cases are floored at ~39–40 µs regardless of `batch_size` (7→64 nearly identical),\n  i.e. **launch/fixed-overhead bound**, essentially independent of the kernel body. Little\n  to gain here; avoid overspending evals (do NOT build split-K / design D unless something\n  changes — H6 stands).\n- Large cases carry the optimization weight and dominate geomean upside. Priority = push\n  the large regime toward the 113 µs floor.\n\n### Next: c002 — `num_warps` sweep, try **16** first (parent c001).\nRationale: at `num_warps=8`, each program (BLOCK_SIZE=4096 fp32) uses 8 elems/thread;\nmore warps (16 → 4 elems/thread) can raise per-SM memory-level parallelism and better\nsaturate HBM on the large regime (H3). Change **only** `num_warps=8→16`; new ID c002.\nIf 16 wins, c003 confirms trend / tries 32 or backs off; if 16 loses, c003 tries 4. Then\nc004 tunes `num_stages` at the best warps. Row-blocking (c005) is the main lever if warp/\nstage tuning stalls below roofline. Stop per Section 6 when large nears ~113 µs or geomean\nconverges.\n","numLines":304,"startLine":1,"totalLines":304}}}
